\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - HKMA says  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  board to stay, banks safe. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Gill Tudor \par \uc2 675 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 1998\par 18:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 1 (Reuters) - Hong Kong is determined to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system and will continue to defend its U.S. dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg against speculative attack, the deputy head of the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  HKMA deputy chief executive David Carse said he saw no threat to the solvency of Hong Kong's banks despite a sharp fall in profits in the first quarter of 1998. \par \par \uc2  "We remain determined to maintain the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system and the current link between the Hong Kong dollar and the U.S. dollar," Carse said in a briefing to a mainly banking audience in London. "There is absolutely no question about that." \par \par \uc2  Abandoning the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, under which the territory's local monetary base must be matched by foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, would provoke huge capital flight, he said. \par \par \uc2  "We are prepared to accept the pain that a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system imposes," he said. \par \par \uc2  Under the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board, interest rates automatically rise if demand for Hong Kong dollars falls. This in turn cramps economic growth and hits property and stock market prices. \par \par \uc2  Carse said the Hong Kong dollar, pegged at 7.8 to the U.S. unit since 1983, had suffered four distinct waves of speculative attack since October 1997 as financial crisis gripped Asia. \par \par \uc2  This had led to extreme interest rate volatility and a higher country risk premium for Hong Hong, with the margin of the one-month Hong Kong Interbank Offered Rate (HIBOR) over the one-month U.S. dollar London Interbank Offered Rate (LIBOR) averaging more than 200 percentage points since mid-1997, he said. \par \par \uc2  In the most recent attack, investors sold short Hong Kong stocks and offloaded Hong Kong dollars in a deliberate ploy to force up interest rates and thus push share prices lower. \par \par \uc2  The HKMA has responded by buying stocks and futures contracts to try to thwart the speculators, spending an estimated HK$100 billion ($12.8 billion) or more. \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng Index lost just under three percent of its value on Tuesday to close at 7,062.47, compared with a high of around 16,800 points in August 1997. \par \par \uc2  The HKMA had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the stock and futures markets on Monday or Tuesday, but was willing to repeat such action if needed, Carse said. \par \par \uc2  "We will come back into the market if it is necessary in order to frustrate this kind of speculation, but we're not going to put any figures on it," he said. \par \par \uc2  "We don't believe it's a breach of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's free market and non-\b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  principles. If anything, our markets have been too free and too easily exploited by people who are not prepared to play by the rules of the game." \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's futures exchange on Saturday announced new rules that would penalise holders of large long and short positions. \par \par \uc2  Although Hong Kong's banking sector was suffering badly from flat lending volumes, lower interest margins and a rise in bad debts, it was still in better shape than banks in other crisis-hit Asian countries such as Indonesia or Thailand, Carse said. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong banks' profits fell by an average of 25 to 30 percent in the first half of 1998, but all remained in profit, he said. \par \par \uc2  Non-performing loans rose sharply to 2.97 percent of the total at the end of June from 1.8 percent at the end of December 1997, he said. \par \par \uc2  "Obviously problem loans are rising in Hong Kong...(but) while asset quality is clearly deteriorating it is not collapsing as it has done in the rest of the region," he said. \par \par \uc2  "The banks remain highly capitalised and liquid. What we're facing is a profitability issue, not a solvency issue. The banks have the strength to manage their way through the current difficulties." ((London Newsroom +44 171 542 6414, fax 583 7239, uk.emergingmarkets.news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du9101zwq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
