\par\par \b \uc2  HK stocks braced for sharp fall after Wall St drop. \b0\par\par\uc2 445 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 1998\par 03:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Sept 1 (Reuters) - Hong Kong stocks are braced for another sharp slide on Tuesday as the market faces the aftermath of a dramatic fall on Wall Street possibly without any support from government buying, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  "We have a dual scenario. One has the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  coming in and keeping the market relatively stable and the other has market forces just playing around with it," said Kent Rossiter, institutional sales manager at Nikko Securities. \par \par \uc2  "I am kind of tempted to say that despite the fact that at some point I do think the HKMA is going to come back in, they should be less concerned about the level of the market as with the pressure on the (Hong Kong) dollar," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Dow industrials suffered their second-largest point loss overnight, tumbling 512.61 points, or 6.37 percent, to 7,539.07 points, rattled by worries about the global economy. \par \par \uc2  Following the drop, the Nikkei 225 was down 378 points, or 2.68 percent, at 13,730 on Tuesday morning. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong market is vulnerable to a sharp correction, having been bolstered by government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the last two weeks. Some brokers expect the Hang Seng Index to return to pre-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels of about 6,500 points. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  could certainly visit the underside of 7,000 today," said Rossiter. \par \par \uc2  As the government retreated from the market on Monday, the Hang Seng Index plummeted 554.70 points, or 7.08 percent, to close at 7,275.04. \par \par \uc2  "It will be down again two or three hundred points," said Geoff Galbraith, vice president institutional sales at DBS Securities. \par \par \uc2  Adding to the bad news, Standard & Poor's on Monday lowered Hong Kong's long-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sovereign credit rating to A from A-plus and cut Hong Kong's short-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating to A-1 from A-1 plus. \par \par \uc2  It said the downgrades reflected a decline in Hong Kong's financial flexibility due to the growing domestic impact of East Asia's recession, which was likely to persist through 1999, as well as the large official purchases in the local equity and futures markets. \par \par \uc2  But Galbraith expected an orderly decline on Tuesday since investors had taken advantage of massive government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday to sell their holdings which would reduce further selling pressure. "All the people in the short term have sold already so there is not going to be too much volume," he said. \par \par \uc2  ((Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2843 6345; Fax (852) 2845 0636)) \par \par \uc2  ((Email: hongkong.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  *. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du9101xcd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
