\par\par \b \uc2  Asia Crisis At A Glance: KLSE Index -11.5% On Cap Controls \b0\par\par\uc2 460 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 1998\par 10:49\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  -- Malaysia's stock index was down 11.5% and continued to fall in afternoon trading in the wake of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's announcement of a number of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and securities trading controls. Ringgit trading all but ground to a halt as Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced sweeping capital controls aimed at deterring speculation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  -- Malaysian Prime Minister Mahathir said that measures announced Tuesday by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  mean that the ringgit has no value outside the country. \par \par \uc2  -- After big losses in the morning following the 6.4% plunge on Wall Street, Tokyo stocks rebounded after government-linked pension funds helped erase the losses by buying stock futures. The Nikkei ended up 261.74, or 1.9%, at 14,369.63. [5011] \par \par \uc2  -- Regional stock markets fell in line with Wall Street's plunge Monday; New Zealand's NZSE-40 capital index ended down 3.7% - its lowest point in five years xrf="nzc00615">; Taiwan shares ended 3.3% lower ; Hong Kong shares ended down 2.9% ; Indonesian shares were 4.7% lower in afternoon trading xrf="jkl00189">; and Singapore shares were down 5.1%. \par \par \uc2  -- The dollar fell to its lowest level against the yen in six weeks before managing a slight comeback. At 0847 GMT, the dollar was at Y138.03, down from Y140.55 late Monday in New York. Japanese Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Sakakibara said the dollar's sharp fall below Y140.00 was caused by the plunge in New York stocks overnight. The Finance Ministry's international bureau head Kuroda said the correction of a weakening yen trend has started. \par \par \uc2  -- Fitch IBCA cut the ratings of eight Hong Kong banks, reflecting the growing impact of "intensifying economic gloom" in their home market. \par \par \uc2  -- The Hong Kong government criticized S&P's downgrade of the territory's credit rating and defended its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock market. \par \par \uc2  -- Japanese Finance Minister Miyazawa said that generally the government shouldn't interfere in financial markets and should take action only when there are disruptive forces in the markets. \par \par \uc2  -- Japan's Economic Planning Agency head Sakaiya said the Japanese economy could become even more severe in the future. \par \par \uc2  -- Japan's sales of new cars, trucks and buses fell 9.6% in August from August 1997, the Japan Automobile Dealers' Association said. \par \par \uc2  -- China's government is confident its 8.0% growth target can be met, the Foreign Ministry spokesman said. He also said China is fully supportive of the HKMA's recent market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  -- The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  Securities Clearing Co. tightened its rules to make it tougher to speculate in local financial markets, a spokesman said. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 01-09-98 \par \par \uc2  0849GMT \par \par \uc2  1042 4190 55126 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du9107gkt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
