\par\par \b \uc2  Hong Kong Alters Strategy In Its Battle With Speculators \b0\par\par\uc2 719 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 1998\par 14:39\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Adding a new wrinkle to the Hong Kong government's high-profile battle against speculators who are trying to force a devaluation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, the government elected not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock and futures markets Monday. \par \par \uc2  Instead, the government introduced restrictions over the weekend on certain types of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and futures trading, mostly in the areas of short-selling and stock borrowing. With the new measures, speculators suddenly found it more expensive to bet against Hong Kong markets. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile Monday, Standard & Poor's lowered its credit and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ratings for the former British colony. The new ratings - to single-A/A-1 from single-A-plus/A-1-plus for Hong Kong's short-term and long-term debt and to single-A-plus/A-1 from double-A-minus/A-1-plus on the Hong Kong dollar - reflect the growing domestic impact of the Asian financial crisis and the government's recent activity in local equity and futures markets, S&P said. \par \par \uc2  Under new rules governing the futures exchange, market players will have to come up with more cash when buying or selling more than 10,000 futures contracts. In addition, members will have to report and identify anyone holding 250 contracts or more. Previously, the reporting level had been 500 contracts, and market players could remain anonymous. \par \par \uc2  For the past two weeks, the Hong Kong government used \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to buy stocks and futures in addition to propping up the Hong Kong dollar as part of a complex defense against speculators who want to sever the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's link to the U.S. dollar. The government - through the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  - spent billions of dollars worth of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to buy blue-chip stocks, Hang Seng Index futures and the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Many estimate the government spent as much as 110 billion Hong Kong dollars ($14.19 billion) in the two weeks. \par \par \uc2  However, analysts said the action exposed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market's vulnerabilities. Critics say the move has provided speculators with exactly the kind of tactical information they need to try something new. And there was even louder criticism for the cost of the effort. Traders estimate that firms acting for the government spent about $9 billion Friday buying mostly blue-chip stocks, attempting to overcome the global tide of falling markets. Altogether, the government has spent as much as 20% of its hard-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to protect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Because Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is tethered to the U.S. dollar under a modified version of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board, interest rates will rise when the Hong Kong dollar is sold. So the recent speculative attacks on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  have put pressure on the Hong Kong stock market. \par \par \uc2  Armed with the knowledge that higher rates will hurt the stock market, speculators have first sold short stock futures and then sold Hong Kong dollars. Selling short involves the sale of borrowed shares or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The speculators profit by buying back their stock futures at a lower price to repay the lender, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline sparks a rise in interest rates and a decline in the stock market. \par \par \uc2  Because the goverment sat out Monday's session, the Hang Seng fell 554.70 points, or 7.1%, to close at 7275.04. \par \par \uc2  The decision to not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  is noteworthy because \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  financial secretary Donald Tsang Yam-kuen last week hinted the government would continue the buying. And Tung Chee-hwa, the territory's chief executive, on Friday suggested that China might contribute its reserves to the expanding struggle. Wang Zuebing, chairman of the state-owned Bank of China, said he supported \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The battle in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's financial markets underscores a deepening slump in this business capital's economy. Friday, officials said the economy contracted 5% during the second quarter, the worst-ever recorded quarterly decline, and one that made it official that Hong Kong is in a recession. Government economists predicted the economy will shrink 4% for the year, which would be the first annual decline since at least 1961, the year Hong Kong began keeping economic statistics. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du8v017xx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
