\par\par \b \uc2  China says would help HK defend  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  if asked. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Paul Eckert \par \uc2 585 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 1998\par 11:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIJING, Aug 31 (Reuters) - A Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said on Monday Beijing would help Hong Kong defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against speculators only if requested, but declined to say if assistance had already been offered. \par \par \uc2  "The Chinese government will help to stabilise the Hong Kong dollar only at the request of the Hong Kong government," said a spokesman for the People's Bank of China, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The spokesman declined to comment when asked if Beijing had joined the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  government's unprecedented effort to defeat speculators through market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of $140 billion could be a crucial weapon in the fight to defend the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peg with the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Rumours have been swirling around Hong Kong markets that Beijing may have committed some of its cash to help the territory's government, which is thought to have spent more than HK$100 billion ($12.8 billion) over the past two weeks buying stocks and futures contracts to thwart speculators. \par \par \uc2  A second \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said China had been helping Hong Kong support its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the biggest threat since the peg was introduced in 1983, but not through direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets. \par \par \uc2  Beijing's aid took the form of expanding credit to Hong Kong companies through branches of Chinese state-owned banks in the territory, where they are significant players. \par \par \uc2  "China began to take part in the campaign to jointly defend the Hong Kong dollar at the end of last year," said the official, who spoke on condition of anonymity. \par \par \uc2  "Hong Kong's economy has been severely hit by the Asian financial crisis and its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also under heavy attack, but it still appears stable," he said. \par \par \uc2  Li Guobin, a top economist with the State Council Information Centre, said Beijing's pledge not to devalue its yuan was already a big prop for Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and would stay that way because a stable yuan was in China's interest. \par \par \uc2  "There shouldn't be a problem of yuan devaluation before the year 2000," he told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether the Hong Kong government needed help from Beijing, Li said: "The Hong Kong government is fully capable of handling its own problem." \par \par \uc2  However, he added: "Premier Zhu Rongji has made it clear that Beijing would help Hong Kong at the request of the Hong Kong government." \par \par \uc2  China regained sovereignty over Hong Kong on July 1, 1997, and is committed to allowing the former British colony a high degree of autonomy under a policy of "one country, two systems". \par \par \uc2  However, a collapse of economic confidence in the capitalist enclave would be a serious blow to China's prestige. \par \par \uc2  Li said the fate of the Hong Kong dollar, the only Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  beside the Chinese yuan to withstand the region's financial meltdown, was in the hands of Hong Kong's people. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the Hong Kong dollar will devalue unless Hong Kongers dump Hong Kong dollars," he said. \par \par \uc2  Li said the Hong Kong government and Hong Kong-based enterprises, the main holders of Hong Kong dollars, would not sell Hong Kong dollars at the moment because devaluation was not in their interest. \par \par \uc2  In comments carried by official media on Sunday, Li lashed out at international fund companies attacking the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , calling them "liars with an axe to grind". \par \par \uc2  (US$1=HK$7.8). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8v01usq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
