\par\par \b \uc2  HK Early Hibor Edges Dn, Banks Await Govt  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 415 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 1998\par 05:55\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--Shorter-term \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  interbank rates have edged only slightly lower early Monday from the previous session as banks wait to see if the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  government will repeat last week's aggressive market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Longer-term rates, however, are creeping upward on borrowing from corporates, money market dealers said, while one dealer with a major foreign institution attributed the increased Hong Kong dollar borrowing to large investment banks. \par \par \uc2  The volatile overnight Hong Kong Interbank Offered Rate is quoted between 18-19%, down a touch from 20% late Friday; the benchmark three-month rate is 15-16%, from last Friday's 16%. \par \par \uc2  Bankers said that short-term rates will likely remain at high levels in quiet trading as banks keep lending to a minimum as they wait to see whether the government will return to the markets. They're also nervously eying the Hang Seng Index, which has plunged by more than 400 points so far Monday, without government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support it. \par \par \uc2  Lending in the six-month and one-year money markets is more active on investment bank borrowing, said the dealer. However, another money market dealer with a European bank described the longer-term borrowing as normal corporate activity. \par \par \uc2  The six-month Hibor is quoted at 16.5%, compared with 14% late Friday. The one-year rate is quoted at 13.5%, from 13% Friday. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the government is believed to have spent up to HK$70.0 billion and billions more in the two weeks before that, to thwart \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators. The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't pumped liquidity into extremely tight money markets in several weeks, in order to avoid giving banks money to fund speculators. \par \par \uc2  Early Monday, the Hong Kong dollar is quoted at HK$7.7435 per U.S. dollar, stronger than the HK$7.7495 recorded late Friday. \par \par \uc2  It's unclear whether banks will ease lending in the short-term or whether the activity in longer-term money markets will continue, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "It really depends on the government," said a Treasury head with a foreign bank. "I can't predict (interbank) rates." \par \par \uc2  The three-month September Hibor futures contract was last traded at 87.00 at 0328 GMT (11:28 p.m. Sunday EDT), implying an interest rate of 13%. The futures quote is down from last Friday's close of 87.10. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 31-08-98 \par \par \uc2  0355GMT \par \par \uc2  1085 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8v07ecz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
