\par\par \b \uc2  Hong Kong stock market war far from over - analysts. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kristina Zetterlund \par \uc2 623 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 1998\par 16:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Aug 28 (Reuters) - The Hong Kong government claimed victory on Friday in its battle against speculators but analysts said the war was not finished yet. \par \par \uc2  "This battle is far from over," Peter Perkins, regional equity strategist at Daiwa Institute of Research told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  "We are going to wake up Monday morning and the speculators or purported speculators and the government are still going to be going at it." \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng Index dropped 93.23 points, or 1.18 percent, on Friday to end at 7,829.74. \par \par \uc2  Turnover reached a record HK$79.0 billion ($10.5 billion), trouncing the previous high of HK$46.02 billion set on August 29, 1997, as the government continued its aggressive buying spree. \par \par \uc2  The blue chip index has risen 17.56 percent since the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) started to buy stocks and index futures on August 14 to fend off \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators. \par \par \uc2  Brokers estimated the government has spent more than HK$100 billion in the past two weeks, using its ammunition to drive out speculators ahead of the expiry of the August Hang Seng Index futures contract on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "We have frustrated their plan," said Financial Secretary Donald Tsang. \par \par \uc2  But speculators were still on the warpath, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The government was successful in squeezing some speculators by widening the gap between the August and September futures contracts, making it costly to roll over positions. \par \par \uc2  The HKMA was also seen tightening money market liquidity to make borrowing more costly. \par \par \uc2  But some investors were still betting that the market would sink to lower depths after the August futures expiry, doubting the government's ability to hold up the index, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Some bears had rolled over their short positions in the August contract into September despite high costs and short selling on individual stocks amounted to HK$7.26 billion on Friday, compared to an already high HK$1.73 billion on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  But analysts raised a warning finger, saying the government was likely to do the unexpected to keep speculators on their toes. On Monday, the wide gap between the cash and futures market was expected to shrink to prevent heavy arbitrage. \par \par \uc2  The September contract closed on Friday at a hefty discount to the cash market, shedding 400 points to 7,210. \par \par \uc2  "Either the cash market comes down or the futures contract goes up, but either way there will be a narrowing of that spread," said Steven Thompson, chief analyst at Nikko Research Center. He said the government would probably cover some of its short positions in the September contract to drive up futures. \par \par \uc2  However, its actions had already inflated the Hang Seng Index to artificial levels which did not reflect the poor state of the local economy, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "They are clearly being blown off course by problems we are seeing around the world," Mark Simpson, head of Hong Kong research at Nomura International, told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  "It is a bit of a living nightmare for both investors and the government at this point," he added. \par \par \uc2  The government said on Friday it was prepared to take further action in the stock and futures market and would follow its heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with policy measures to enhance \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's resilience to speculative attacks. \par \par \uc2  "We did not mount the market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced a fortnight ago in haste. They were part of an overall strategy to strengthen our financial markets," Tsang told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  "These measures should reduce the need of market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  once they have been put in place firmly." \par \par \uc2  ((\b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  newsroom (852) 2843 6358; Fax (852) 2845 0636, hongkong.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8s01r24\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
