\par\par \b \uc2  HK  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  hurts rather than helps peg system. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrea Ricci \par \uc2 821 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 1998\par 04:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Aug 28 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the territory's stock and futures market may imperil the very system it seeks to save - the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board. \par \par \uc2  "It seems to me that the government's action is evidence of its increasing fears about the consequences of keeping the peg," said Richard Margolis, first vice president, equity research, at Merrill Lynch (Asia Pacific). \par \par \uc2  In the past, authorities' "balance of fear" between the risks of keeping the peg and letting it go clearly was on the side of maintaining the link. \par \par \uc2  "What they've done seems to suggest that the calculation is no longer as solid as it was. And if this process continues, it may be they are moving towards a stage in which the balance would shift in favour of letting (the peg) go," he said. \par \par \uc2  Since 1983, the Hong Kong dollar has been linked to the U.S. dollar at HK$7.8 per US$1 through a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system. \par \par \uc2  Repeated speculative attacks on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the backdrop of Asia's financial crisis have kept interest rates at punishingly high levels over the past year. \par \par \uc2  Higher interest rates have sent asset prices reeling. \par \par \uc2  By August 13, the blue-chip Hang Seng Index had fallen almost 60 percent from its peak in August 1997. \par \par \uc2  On August 14, the government took action against what it called a "double play" game by speculators - who had sold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  not because Hong Kong was fundamentally weak or even because they had a perverse desire to break the peg - but to take advantage of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system. \par \par \uc2  Under the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board, pressures for a weaker Hong Kong dollar automatically cause rates to increase and vice versa. \par \par \uc2  By selling the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , speculators could be sure interest rates would rise and could be fairly certain share prices would fall, which meant easy profits on short stock positions. \par \par \uc2  Analysts initially greeted the move with scepticism and the view has become increasingly negative as the days go by. \par \par \uc2  "The idea behind this was to take some of the pressure off interest rates because they regarded some of the pressure to be unjustified and caused by speculators, and on that basis (Hong Kong authorities) haven't succeeded," Margolis said. \par \par \uc2  "And if you arrive at the conclusion that what you are doing is worse than than allowing the existing mechanism to do its job, then it is best to leave the existing mechanism alone." \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's leaders have said over and over again that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg was a pillar of strength in a weakened Asia and that it would be maintained, even if higher interest rates resulted in painfully lower asset prices. \par \par \uc2  But now the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) is propping up share prices, while also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the money markets to squeeze speculators needing to fund short positions. \par \par \uc2  Last month, the HKMA bypassed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system by purchasing Hong Kong dollars for the treasury to fund a fiscal shortfall. The action was perfectly legitimate, but nevertheless raised questions about the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's motives. \par \par \uc2  "The strategy they are pursuing basically takes the peg off autopilot and makes the credibility of the peg a function of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  abilities of the HKMA, rather than a system that is self-enforcing and self-sustaining," said Cliff Tan, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Warburg Dillon Read. \par \par \uc2  Lehman Brothers economist Rob Subbaraman said the stock market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  set a bad precedent. "It's not how a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board was meant to operate," he said. \par \par \uc2  "Either the HKMA is geared on punishing speculators and that's its pure motive. Or it can be a sign that the HKMA is becoming desperate," Subbaraman said. \par \par \uc2  "The economy is weakening and they can't afford to put up interest rates and let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board operate fully because they are afraid if the stock market falls, it could cause the banks to get into trouble. And of course the ultimate way for the peg to be unravelled is through a banking crisis," he said. \par \par \uc2  In the end, the action would raise Hong Kong's risk premium because of uncertainty about whether the government would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And that would not be good for the peg. \par \par \uc2  One economist at a European investment bank said that before the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  he believed the peg was sufficiently de-politicised to be self-sustaining. But that has changed. \par \par \uc2  "Now when I get up in the morning, I won't be saying that the Hong Kong dollar is foolproof. I'll be thinking, what is going on externally? What's happening in the domestic economy? And are these things pushing enough of the HKMA's buttons that they will actually break the peg?" \par \par \uc2  - Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2843-6371; Fax (852) 2845-0636 \par \par \uc2  - Email: hongkong.newsroom@reuters.com. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8s01py4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
