\par\par \b \uc2  Asia Crisis/Glance:Tokyo Stks At 12-Yr Low; HK In Recession \b0\par\par\uc2 480 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 1998\par 10:33\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  -- Concerns about global financial turmoil, reaction to the U.S. stock sell-off overnight and jitters about economic and financial problems at home triggered heavy selling on the Tokyo Stock Exchange, as the Nikkei average fell 3.4% to 13,915.63, its lowest close since March 1986. Finance Minister Miyazawa urged stock market participants to remain calm. Senior Japanese officials said the government shouldn't try to manipulate stock prices. \par \par \uc2  -- Hong Kong acknowledged recession, as it reported that the economy shrank by 5% in the second quarter, following a drop of 2.8% in the first quarter. The government forecast a GDP contraction for the year of 4%. \par \par \uc2  -- At 0810 GMT, the dollar was at Y143.03, sharply up from Y141.87 late Thursday in New York. The yen rose against the dollar earlier in Asian trading, on overseas investors' profit-taking to make up for huge losses suffered from investment in emerging markets. \par \par \uc2  -- Director General of Japan's EPA Sakaiya said GDP for the April-June and July-September quarters may show negative growth as economic conditions are "extremely severe." \par \par \uc2  -- Asian stock markets fell after Wall Street's worst performance this year. The Philippine index closed 5.7% lower, its lowest closing level in more than six years; ; New Zealand shares ended 3.3% lower, with the NZSE-40 index at its lowest level in five years; and Taiwan shares ended down 1.3% at a 22-month low. Also, Indonesia's stock index was 4.0% lower, Malaysia's 1.5% and Thailand's 3.2% midafternoon. \par \par \uc2  -- Heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government helped prevent \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  stocks from a sharp fall; the Hang Seng Index closed down 1.2%. \par \par \uc2  -- The volatility in Hong Kong's stock and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets is indirectly the result of U.S. and Japanese reluctance to prop up the yen, a Chinese newspaper editorial said. \par \par \uc2  -- George Soros' Quantum Fund joined speculators betting against the Hong Kong dollar, Hong Kong newspapers reported. \par \par \uc2  -- The Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor and his deputy resigned, one day after the bank reported that the economy shrank by 6.8% in the second quarter. Prime Minister Mahathir said the worst is over for his nation's economy. \par \par \uc2  -- Japan's jobless rate in July fell 0.2 percentage point to 4.1% from June's all-time high 4.3%, but the number of employed fell for the sixth straight month. \par \par \uc2  -- Finance Minister Miyazawa told the Diet that Long-Term Credit Bank of Japan will fail without an injection of public funds, Nihon Keizai Shimbun reported. \par \par \uc2  -- The crisis in Russia won't harm the IMF's commitment to Indonesia, but it does pose a threat to Asia as a whole, Indonesian Senior Economics Minister Ginandjar said. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 28-08-98 \par \par \uc2  0833GMT \par \par \uc2  1042 4190 55126 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8s07bmu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
