\par\par \b \uc2  Asia Crisis At A Glance:Tokyo Stks Plunge On Global Worries \b0\par\par\uc2 499 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 1998\par 10:11\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  -- Tokyo stocks plunged on fears of Russia-led global market turmoil and worries about a delay of Japan's passage of key banking reform bills. The Nikkei ended 3% lower at 14,413.79, its lowest level since August 1992. \par \par \uc2  -- In hopes of a compromise on key financial-reform bills, Japan's ruling LDP began studying counterproposals drawn up by the three main opposition forces, the Nihon Keizai Shimbun reported. \par \par \uc2  -- The yen rose against the dollar despite a sharp fall in Japanese stock prices, as players bought the yen to flee from plunging emerging market assets. At 0810 GMT, the dollar was at Y143.62, down from Y144.07 late Wednesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  -- Japan's production of cars, trucks and buses fell 10.7% in July from July 1997 to 920,776 units, the Japan Automobile Manufacturers' Association said. \par \par \uc2  -- South Korea's GDP shrank by a dismal 6.6% during the second quarter from a year ago, swinging from 6.6% growth in the year-ago period. \par \par \uc2  -- U.S. economist Krugman defended the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  government's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock market, despite criticism it violates \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reputation as a free market economy, the South China Morning Post reported. As \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government continues its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it has targeted a wider range of stocks to include all the blue-chip constituents of the Hang Seng Index, traders said. \par \par \uc2  -- Southeast Asian nations may have to devalue their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  by another 10%-12%, Krugman told Singapore's Straits Times. \par \par \uc2  -- Russia's latest economic woes won't spill over to China's economy and won't change the government's stance of maintaining a stable yuan exchange rate, a spokesman at China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. \par \par \uc2  -- China is still committed to 8% GDP growth this year and has outlined a series of measures to meet that goal, including more bond issues to fund infrastructure projects, state media reported. \par \par \uc2  -- Finance Minister Anwar said Malaysia's second-quarter economic performance will be worse than the 1.8% contraction in the first quarter, national news agency Bernama reported.  The economy could contract more than 2% this year, de facto economic minister Daim said. \par \par \uc2  -- New Zealand's stock index ended down 1.7% at its lowest point in three-and-a-half years, as the local share market reacted negatively to the continuation of global economic uncertainty. \par \par \uc2  -- U.S.-based economist Friedman said Russia and some East Asian nations are in "serious trouble," but their problems mostly will pass if policy makers allow market forces to operate. \par \par \uc2  -- Mobs stole food from shops for a second day in an Indonesian town despite a police warning that looters would be shot, police reported. \par \par \uc2  -- Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Singson said the economy most likely shrank in the second quarter, but the peso shouldn't be affected when the data is released Friday. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 27-08-98 \par \par \uc2  0811GMT \par \par \uc2  1042 4190 55126 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8r07dd3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
