\par\par \b \uc2  Economists Warn HK$ Peg In Danger If  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Continues \b0\par\par\uc2  By Adam S. Najberg \par \uc2 373 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 1998\par 11:33\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar peg's days are numbered if the government continues to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in local markets, more and more economists warn. \par \par \uc2  As recently as a year ago, it was sacrilegious here to discuss the fall of Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board. Last October, legislator and businessman James Tien Pei-chun was castigated by the government and, under pressure from fellow business people, retracted suggestions that it was time to rethink the peg. \par \par \uc2  But the government's repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets has caused a massive change in local sentiment, with many analysts now talking openly about the peg's imminent downfall. \par \par \uc2  "The peg's a goner," says Christopher Wood, a global and Asian strategist at Santander Investment Securities Asia Ltd. in Hong Kong. "My view is that it's likely to go by the end of the year." \par \par \uc2  Many economists say the demise of the 15-year-old U.S. dollar link is being hastened every time the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  spends money in the stock, stock futures and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to defend it. They argue that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's markets is counter-productive because it prevents the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system from functioning properly. \par \par \uc2  Some even speculate that Hong Kong Chief Executive Tung Chee-hwa will use an Oct. 15 policy speech to unveil a new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  arrangement, despite the government's repeated statements that it will defend the peg at nearly any cost. \par \par \uc2  Jim Mellon, chairman of hedge fund specialist Regent Pacific Holdings (H.RPG), says the peg could go before the end of the year, with a devaluation of the Chinese yuan acting as a catalyst for its demise. Although he says the latest market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has succeeded in driving away speculators, he warns \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't solve some basic problems \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  is experiencing. \par \par \uc2  The essence of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board is a governmental surrender of monetary control, letting markets determine interest rates, Wood notes. If speculators sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , interest rates rise and asset prices fall, eventually attracting buyers back into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 27-08-98 \par \par \uc2  0933GMT \par \par \uc2  1144 4203 55127 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8r079xt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
