\par\par \b \uc2  Barclays Capital urges caution on HK firms' bonds. \b0\par\par\uc2 475 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 1998\par 11:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Aug 26 (Reuters) - Barclays Capital said on Wednesday it was reversing its earlier positive stance toward bond issues of Hong Kong firms and urging investors to refrain from entering the market. \par \par \uc2  "Many Hong Kong bonds appear cheap based on their apparent creditworthiness," Barclays said in a report. \par \par \uc2  "The recent moves from the HKMA (Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) and trends in emerging markets in general, though, skew the probabilities toward further widening of bond spreads," it said. \par \par \uc2  Offshore bonds of Hong Kong issuers were investment grade but nevertheless had been trading as if they were at a high risk of default, Barclays said. \par \par \uc2  For example, Wharf Holdings BBB-plus/Baa3 rated bond due in March 2003 was trading at 900 basis points over comparable U.S. Treasuries. \par \par \uc2  Hutchison Whampoa's Single-A/A3 bond due August 2007 was trading at 425 basis points over the benchmark. \par \par \uc2  "Many Hong Kong companies remain strong credits and can withstand severe downturns in the economy. However, fundamentals do not appear to matter at the moment," Barclays said. \par \par \uc2  Despite the Hong Kong government's recent efforts to deter attacks on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in stock, futures and curency markets, further \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  attacks were likely, it said \par \par \uc2  "Recent events in Russia and growing pressure on Venezuela's bolivar will encourage \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators that the Hong Kong dollar too is a vulnerable target," Barclays said. \par \par \uc2  "Some even see the HKMA's recent actions as 'desperate', signalling increased prospects of a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," it said. \par \par \uc2  If pressure continues on the Hong Kong dollar, interest rates would remain high, slowing the economy further and making local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt more difficult to service. \par \par \uc2  This would hurt most Hong Kong issuers, which in turn could face further spread widening on their offshore bonds. \par \par \uc2  Bonds also were likely to suffer once the HKMA stopped supporting the stock market because of the strong relationship between \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  stocks and bonds. \par \par \uc2  "The HKMA cannot \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock market ad infinitum. Therefore, there is a danger that when it does stop supporting the stock market, the index could slump, irrespective of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and interest rates," Barclays said. \par \par \uc2  Barclays said the first step in a sustained contraction in Hong Kong bond spreads would be the perception of greater \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  certainty. The second would be falling interest rates. \par \par \uc2  If \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  attacks abate, Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board would ensure that interest rates fell, Barlclays said. \par \par \uc2  If the peg were abandoned, short-term rates might initially spike to offset capital flows, but would ease several months later, it said. \par \par \uc2  "We believe when \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and interest rate views stabilise, it will provide attractive entry levels for the top Hong Kong credits with only limited downside risk," it said. \par \par \uc2  - Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2843-6371; Fax (852) 2845-0636. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \