\par\par \b \uc2  AWSJ: HKMA  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Creates Ripples: Fund Managers \b0\par\par\uc2 454 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 1998\par 01:47\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  From Thursday's Asian Wall Street Journal \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    By Laurie Lande\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--As the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues buying blue-chip stocks to ward off \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators, some local fund managers say they'd rather stay away from these shares than guess where the HKMA is going to jump in next. \par \par \uc2  "It really is a guessing game," says Nick Cringle, senior fund manager at Coutts Bank. "The trend we've seen this week is that they (the HKMA) rotate among batches of stocks for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It makes it difficult for fund managers to decide where there are good buys." As a result, while managers may do some selective buying, there "isn't a huge amount of buying and selling going on." \par \par \uc2  Giampaolo Guarnieri, director of Asia Pacific Equities at Salomon Smith Barney, also says he's trying to stay on the sidelines as long as the government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues. \par \par \uc2  "We've been cutting our exposure to \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he says. "We had started to buy blue chips the week before they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But I'm happy to give away some of my blue chips if there is a willing buyer, and there is - the HKMA." \par \par \uc2  A fair number of fund managers appear to be following this strategy. Notes Terence Mahoney, managing director at TCW Asia, the Asian arm of U.S.-based TCW Funds Management Inc.: "A lot of people were looking to buy the Hang Seng (Index) at the 6000 level, but now that the index is higher, they'll be looking to lighten their load and make a profit," he says. On Wednesday, the index fell 55.69 points to 7834.40. \par \par \uc2  "We're not making any aggressive moves at the moment," adds Henry Tse, investment manager at Indocam Asia Asset Management, which specializes in Hong Kong and China funds. "If prices rise, and we feel certain stocks are overvalued, that may trigger some selling, but there's no point in being too aggressive at this stage as the market could go even higher." \par \par \uc2  Small fund managers are even more likely to stay on the sidelines rather than gamble on where the HKMA will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  next. Notes Henry Lee, manager at Hendale Asia, "Smaller funds have to be careful ... you end up pushed to the sidelines." \par \par \uc2  A main reason for some managers staying out is the difficulty of making accurate stock valuations in an environment in which government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - rather than a company's fundamentals - is moving stock prices. \par \par \uc2  "Traditional analysis may not work in this type of environment, where \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is distorting the value of blue chips," explains Tse of Indocam. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 26-08-98 \par \par \uc2  2347GMT \par \par \uc2  62544 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8q07bhz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
