\par\par \b \uc2  HK Dollar Stronger Late Amid Covering Of Short Positions \b0\par\par\uc2 294 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 1998\par 11:59\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The Hong Kong dollar strengthened sharply Tuesday, as speculators scrambled to unload short positions in the face of rising local interest rates, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0944 GMT (5:44 a.m. EDT), the Hong Kong dollar is quoted at HK$7.7457 against the U.S. dollar, stronger than HK$7.7480 quoted late Monday. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has traded in a range of HK$7.7423 and HK$7.7473 so far Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "All the focus" is on interbank lending rates, said one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader. Late Tuesday, the volatile overnight Hong Kong Interbank Offered Rate was quoted at 12.00%, compared with 10.00% late in the previous session; it had been near 16% earlier in the day. The three-month rate was quoted at 13.00%, up from 12.00% late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the higher interest rates made it more expensive to maintain speculative short positions against the Hong Kong dollar and local equities, prompting some speculators to buy the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market to cover their positions in the futures market. \par \par \uc2  For more Hibor news, please refer to Telerate page \par \par \uc2  Market participants said they're less concerned Tuesday about the government's continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  stock market. On Monday, government buying drove the Hang Seng Index up 4.2%. \par \par \uc2  While traders said they saw signs of government buying again Tuesday, the effort was milder, with the Hang Seng Index gaining only 0.05%, to close at 7,890.09. \par \par \uc2  By driving up share prices, the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  hopes to hurt speculators who have been shorting stocks and the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 25-08-98 \par \par \uc2  0959GMT \par \par \uc2  1053 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8p078vm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
