\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS - Govt action in Asia rings alarms. \b0\par\par\uc2 826 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 1998\par 04:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  By Valerie Lee SINGAPORE, Aug 23 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by some Asian governments to support their stock markets and fend off speculators has received the thumbs-down from most analysts, who say governments should keep clear of markets or risk getting their fingers burned. \par \par \uc2  Investors gave a clear signal late last week, taking profit after sharp rallies in markets in Kuala Lumpur, Hong Kong and Taiwan earlier in the week on talk of official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At the close of trade on Friday, several Asian markets had surrendered the week's gains with Malaysia's key Composite Index tumbling 7.69 percent as investors cashed out. \par \par \uc2  "Obviously, investors will be disappointed because they hate surprise elements and if the governments try to manage the market it is not taken very positively," said Nilesh Jasani, a regional strategist with SG Securities in Singapore. "The alarm bells always go off a bit when you hear about governments propping up the markets because you are never quite sure where the real floor is," said Peter Douglas, Director of Aberdeen Asset Management in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said government-backed buying of shares would bring out sellers eager to liquidate their holdings at the expense of the regional authorities. It could also hurt the long-term investor outlook for the markets involved, as risk premiums for these markets would rise, they added. \par \par \uc2  "What is more, you are not addressing the real issues, you are just swirling money around in the economy," said Kevin Gin, a director with Kleinwort Benson Securities. "It's taking money from out of one pocket and putting it into another," he said. \par \par \uc2  In recent weeks, several Asian governments either stepped into their battered share markets as buyers or announced their intention to use public funds to stimulate markets that are drastically weaker than they were before the Asian financial crisis flared up a year ago. \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  started things, repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in stocks and future markets after leader Tung Chee-hwa said the government would step in if speculators attacked the Hong Kong dollar and pushed interest rates higher while holding short stock positions. \par \par \uc2  In Malaysia, Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad said last week that a fund that subsidises the annual pilgrimages of Malaysian Muslims to Saudi Arabia might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy shares on behalf of the government. \par \par \uc2  Malaysian Deputy Prime Minister Anwar Ibrahim said the huge Employees' Pension Fund (EPF), could also step in. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan also said its securities watchdog, the Securities and Futures Commission, was drafting various proposals to stabilise the stock market. \par \par \uc2  Investors said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might succeed as the government was not committed to defending a specific level on the key Hang Seng share index. But the win would not come without a price. "It is not coming cheap. What it means is that the government is not going through the mechanism of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board," said a European investment house strategist, who declined to be named. \par \par \uc2  "It is going outside it, which means that risk premiums on Hong Kong in future will go up," he said, adding that the country's reputation as perhaps the freest financial market in Asia had been compromised. \par \par \uc2  Another issue was also whether or not the regional authorities had deep enough pockets to sustain their support of the markets as sellers continue to emerge, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "...the market will cap any artificially generated boost to the asset markets," London-based research house I.D.E.A. said in its regional markets focus on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "In the near-term, some caution may be evident in shorting the indices and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  but over a longer time frame (bearish) fundamentals are seen winning through," it said. \par \par \uc2  But at least one investor said regional authorities were entitled to take protective measures. \par \par \uc2  "A government has a social objective at the end of the day. If you are a government, you have been elected, you have a social objective and if investors are getting unreasonable you have to act," said Jean Paolo Guarnieri, a director with Salomon Brothers Asset Management in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "All this excitement from investors against the government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a little bit naive. When you deal in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets around the world, for example, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is always there....the objective of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not always mercantile," he said. \par \par \uc2      Percentage change in index levels since start of Asia crisis: \par \par \uc2  Market............Current.........July 1997.........Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Indonesia\tab    388.35\tab     731.00\tab \tab -46.9 pct\par Malaysia\tab     324.06\tab     1230.00\tab      -73.7 pct\par Philippines\tab 1372.60\tab     2815.00\tab      -51.2 pct\par Singapore\tab    933.16\tab     1981.00\tab      -52.9 pct\par Thailand\tab     238.92\tab      569.00\tab      -58.0 pct\par Hong Kong\tab   7527.61\tab    15055.00\tab      -50.0 pct\par Japan\tab      15298.20\tab    200