\par\par \b \uc2  FUND VIEW - Hong Kong cannot turn back Asian tide. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sarah Davison \par \uc2 666 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 August 1998\par 09:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Aug 23 (Reuters) - Government \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock market have provided some support for \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's battered shares, but such moves are no long-term solution to economic woes plaguing the territory and the region, financial analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The government bought shares last week in a bid to foil speculators, pushing up the Hang Seng index and cheering markets in some other beleaguered Asian centres. \par \par \uc2  But the joy was largely short lived as many investors, unsettled by growing weakness in overseas markets, took advantage of the rally to sell their shares and book profits. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong is expected to renew its battles with speculators this week as it tries to bolster sentiment amid a slew of economic data expected to confirm it is in recession. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the territory's government alone could not turn back the Asian tide, despite tough words from Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  boss Joseph Yam. \par \par \uc2  Yam was correct when he said Asian market declines were an over-reaction that bore no relation to any reasonable assessment of Asia's prospects, said Don Hanna, economist at Goldman Sachs. \par \par \uc2  "But Hong Kong, in itself, can't do much about that," Hanna said. \par \par \uc2  Global markets are showing every sign of becoming more unstable as the Asian crisis drags on, adding to the bearishness in the region, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Last week, Wall Street started to wobble as some Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  looked vulnerable and Russia grappled with financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  "I think the government was absolutely right to get involved. Given the peg, they couldn't do anything else," said Chris Tinker, head of regional economics at ING Barings. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar is the last \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia linked to the U.S. dollar and the government has vowed to defend the peg. \par \par \uc2  Some speculators are believed to be attacking the dollar in order to reap profits on stocks. \par \par \uc2  They sell stocks and then move to attack the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . When banks raise interest rates to defend the dollar, corporate profit outlooks and stock prices usually fall. Speculators can then buy back the shares at a much lower price. Hong Kong is now poised for battle with the speculators, with the nearest Hang Seng index futures contract due to expire at the end of the week. \par \par \uc2  It is an important battle, given the uncertainty shaking global markets. There are widespread fears that a break of the Hong Kong dollar peg - considered impossible unless the local populace loses all confidence and switches deposits into other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  - could have worldwide implications. \par \par \uc2  Ultimately, however, underlying issues such as debt must be addressed to quell Asia's economic turmoil, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The only solution to Asia's problems and the growing volatility in global emerging markets is a write-down of Asian debt by global banks, said Chris Tinker, head of regional economics at ING Barings. \par \par \uc2  "The counterparties that have to admit culpability here are the banks," Tinker said. "Banks have to accept responsibility." \par \par \uc2  Tinker said the failure of global banks to take the hit on the Asian crisis had kept risk premiums high and prevented Asia from attracting the capital it needed to stabilise. \par \par \uc2  "By failing to write down debt and therefore maintaining the risk premium demanded on Asian assets, they are preventing the opportunity cost of investment in Asia from falling," he said. \par \par \uc2  That has reduced net capital inflows into Asian markets and sustained volatility caused by reduced liquidity, Tinker said. \par \par \uc2  For as long as the banks refuse to admit culpability for the bad lending decisions that produced the Asian crisis, Asia will remain a source of global instability, he said. \par \par \uc2  "The issue isn't whether or not capital markets or financial markets are more volatile, the issue is what you can do about it and whose responsibility it is," Hanna said. \par \par \uc2  - Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2843 6470; Fax 2845 0636 \par \par \uc2  - hongkong.newsroom@reuters.com. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8n01cnb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
