\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong \highlight\uc2  Stocks Fall 3% As Government Halts Market  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 746 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 August 1998\par 10:45\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  shares snapped a two-day winning streak to close lower almost 3% Friday, as investors cashed in on recent gains and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  government took a breather from its high-profile \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up local share prices. \par \par \uc2  The blue-chip Hang Seng index fell 214.92 points, or 2.8%, to end at 7527.61, after jumping 16% in the past four sessions on government buying of selected blue chips. Thursday, it rose 119.95 points, or 1.6%. \par \par \uc2  Trading volume shrank to HK$4.97 billion from Thursday's HK$7.03 billion. The broader All Ordinaries index shed 73.64 points to close at 3355.29. \par \par \uc2  "There's some profit-taking today (Friday), and some people are squaring their positions before the weekend," said Andrew To, dealing director at AMB Securities HK Ltd. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong government had been using \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to buy stocks and futures in addition to propping up the Hong Kong dollar as part of a complex defense against speculators who want to sever the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's link to the U.S. dollar. The government - through the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  - had been spending \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to buy blue-chip stocks, Hang Seng index futures and the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Two traders from local brokerage houses who declined to be named said they suspected the government also unloaded some shares Friday, pocketing some profits and saving ammunition for the battle with hedge funds next week when the August index futures contract expires. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, a weakening yen drove up local interbank rates and soured buying interest in equity market, said a trader with a local brokerage firm. \par \par \uc2  The volatile overnight Hong Kong Interbank Offered Rate edged up to 9.00% late Friday from 8.875% late Thursday, while the three-month rate climbed to 10.50% from 10.125% late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  A sharp sell-off in the futures market also helped to drag down the underlying index, said the trader with the local brokerage house. \par \par \uc2  The August Hang Seng index futures contract fell 400 points to end at 7390, a 137-point discount to the cash market. Around 21,648 contracts changed hands. \par \par \uc2  The number of open interest for both August and September reached 103,299. It has been closely watched by market participants since the government declared an all-out war against speculators in equity and equity derivative markets last Friday. \par \par \uc2  Major blue-chip stocks led the across-the-board decline Friday, after recording hefty gains in recent sessions. \par \par \uc2  British bank giant HSBC Holdings PLC topped the most-active list, shedding HK$6.00 to HK$165.00 amid volume valued at HK$1.10 billion. \par \par \uc2  Other actively-traded stocks included China Telecom (Hong Kong) Ltd., which lost 95 cents to HK$10.40; Conglomerate Hutchison Whampoa Ltd., down 90 cents to HK$38.00; and Hong Kong Telecommunications Ltd., shedding 15 cents to HK$14.85. \par \par \uc2  Major developers' shares also took a beating. Cheung Kong (Holdings) Ltd. lost 60 cents to HK$33.00 while Sun Hung Kai Properties Ltd. edged down 10 cents to HK$25.05. Conglomerate Swire Pacific Ltd. shed HK$1.45 to HK$22.35. \par \par \uc2  China-related shares fared no better, with the red-chip and H share indexes falling 3.3% and 2.9%, respectively, to end at 629.44 and 284.87. Red chips are Hong Kong-listed companies backed by mainland government entities while H shares are Chinese state-owned enterprises listed in the territory. Conglomerate China Resources Enterprise Ltd. fell 30 cents to HK$5.40; Mainland toll road operator Jiangsu Expressway Co. lost 13%, or 19 cents, to HK$1.27. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, Cheung Kong Infrastructure Holdings Ltd. bucked the trend to close up 35 cents at HK$13.90, after reporting Thursday that net profit rose 20% to HK$1.23 billion in the first half ended June 30 from the same period a year earlier. Its competitor, New World Infrastructure Ltd., however, was among the top losers in percentage terms, falling 11%, or 65 cents, to HK$5.05. \par \par \uc2  Traders predicted more volatility in the market next week when the government is expected to resume its war against the hedge funds. "The battle is going to be very fierce," warned one trader with an Asian brokerage house. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du8l015r8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
