\par\par \b \uc2  HK Tung -4: Says "HK People Will Bear This Pain" \b0\par\par\uc2 375 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 1998\par 08:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  "We need a breathing spell," where interest rates may be a bit high but are predictable, Tung said, explaining the government's meddling in local markets. \par \par \uc2  Tung also defended the government's decision to protect the Hong Kong dollar's peg to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "A stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  environment is important to a small economy like Hong Kong's," Tung said, though he conceded that maintaining the peg will come at a heavy price, including high interest rates and a sharp correction in asset prices. \par \par \uc2  "Asset prices needed a correction anyway," to be competitive, Tung said. "Rates may be high but at least they're stable and predictable." \par \par \uc2  "Hong Kong people will bear this pain," the chief executive said. \par \par \uc2  Tung said if Hong Kong were to abandon the peg, there would be massive capital flight and rates would soar. \par \par \uc2  "It would be wrong for us to embark on such uncharted waters," Tung said. \par \par \uc2  Tung also defended the government suspension of land sales, though some criticized the move as another interference with the free-market system. \par \par \uc2  The objective was for "prices to achieve some stability because an unstable situation will harm our banking system," he said. \par \par \uc2  Tung said the government is currently reviewing its housing policy "very seriously" in light of the financial "turmoil around us." \par \par \uc2  Tung said the Asian turmoil isn't going to just hit emerging markets in Asia and Eastern Europe, describing it as "a global problem that really needs a global solution." \par \par \uc2  If the Asian turmoil isn't be addressed, it will ultimately "impact the rest of the world," the chief executive said. \par \par \uc2  Tung's comments follow a strong defense of the government's stock market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief executive Joseph Yam, writing in the editorial page of the Asian Wall Street Journal's Thursday edition. \par \par \uc2  Yam said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was meant to send a "very clear message to those manipulating our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for this purpose (to profit from a short position in the stock market) that they may stand to lose money instead. But if there were no \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation there would be no such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Yam said. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 20-08-98 \par \par \uc2  0636GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8k072yx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
