\par\par \b \uc2  HKMA Yam Calls On G-7 To Re-Think Non- \highlight22\uc2 Interventionist \highlight\uc2  Stance \b0\par\par\uc2 174 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 1998\par 04:47\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--The head of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday called on the Group of Seven economies to re-think their stance that non-\b \highlight22\uc2 interventionism\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the way to maintain global financial-markets and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability, according to a newspaper report. \par \par \uc2  Defending the Hong Kong government's recent forays into the stock, stock futures and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to repel speculators, \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chief Executive Joseph Yam said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is sometimes necessary to protect an economy. To not do so, to allow speculators to deflate income levels and push unemployment higher, will have global consequences, he said. \par \par \uc2  "I think there is now a need to get a message to the G-7 that abstaining from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain global financial stability, particularly \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability, may no longer be a viable option," Yam said in a column carried on the Asian Wall Street Journal's editorial page Thursday. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 20-08-98 \par \par \uc2  0247GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8k072du\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
