\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong \highlight\uc2  Stocks Gain 1.6% Amid Continued Government  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 714 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 1998\par 11:03\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  shares closed almost 2% higher Thursday amid the government's continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. \par \par \uc2  The blue-chip Hang Seng index rose 119.95 points, or 1.6%, to 7742.53, adding to Wednesday's 411.66-point gain. \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng index has soared 16% in the past four trading days since last Friday. The market was closed for a holiday Monday. \par \par \uc2  Propelled by heavy short-covering of blue chips, market volume swelled to HK$7.03 billion from Wednesday's HK$6.94 billion. The broader All Ordinaries index gained 47.19 points to 3428.93. \par \par \uc2  "It was mostly futures-led" buying, said Jerry Pang, equity salesman at HSBC Securities Asia Ltd. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong government has been using \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to buy stocks and futures in addition to propping up the Hong Kong dollar as part of a complex defense against speculators who want to sever the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's link to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The government - through the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  - has been spending \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to buy blue-chip stocks, Hang Seng index futures and the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Because Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is tethered to the U.S. dollar under a modified version of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board, interest rates will rise when the Hong Kong dollar is sold. The recent speculative attacks on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  have triggered massive selling in the Hong Kong stock market. \par \par \uc2  Armed with the knowledge that higher rates will hurt the stock market, speculators have first sold short stock futures and then sold Hong Kong dollars. Selling short involves the sale of borrowed shares or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The speculators profit by buying back their stock futures at a lower price, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline sparks a rise in interest rates and a decline in the stock market. \par \par \uc2  Investors holding short positions have been forced to scramble to cover their positions as the index rises. As there are rumors that the government will continue to drive the index above 8000, investors short in the futures market rushed to square their positions before incurring further losses, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "Most buying in the cash market was genuine buying," as opposed to government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Pang said. \par \par \uc2  The yen has also strengthened to around 142.75 yen per U.S. dollar late Thursday, which has been positive for market sentiment, analysts added. \par \par \uc2  After rallying considerably in recent sessions, the Hang Seng index started to consolidate Thursday and some profit-taking emerged around the 7800-point level, which erased some of the index's gains earlier in the session, Pang said. \par \par \uc2  The August Hang Seng Index futures contract rose 125 points from the previous session to 7790. The derivative was trading at a premium to the cash market. \par \par \uc2  HSBC Holdings PLC, the day's most active stock, was up HK$3.50, or 2.1%, to HK$171.00. \par \par \uc2  Shares of conglomerate Hutchison Whampoa Ltd. added 90 cents to HK$38.90, while its parent, Cheung Kong (Holdings) Ltd., was flat at HK$33.60. \par \par \uc2  Shares of two utility giants also rose. China Telecom (Hong Kong) Ltd. gained 25 cents to HK$11.35 and Hong Kong Telecommunications Ltd. rose 30 cents to HK$15.00. \par \par \uc2  A stronger yen helped to relieve pressure for a yuan devaluation, which in turn boosted China-linked shares. The red-chip index was ahead 5.9% and the H-share index climbed 6.3%. Red chips are companies incorporated in Hong Kong but usually controlled by Chinese government entities, with their main \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in China. H-shares are stock of Chinese state-owned enterprises listed in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  Market players predict the government will continue to fiddle with the stock market in the near future as it continues its battle to squeeze speculators out of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock futures markets. \par \par \uc2  "They (government officials) will keep on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  until the end of August," said Alan Hutcheson, research director of Pacific Challenge Securities Ltd. \par \par \uc2  Pang noted that the market "will be highly volatile next week before the futures expiry" next Thursday. He predicted the Hang Seng index will trade in the 7000-8000 region. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du8k0157p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
