\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Malaysia explores ways of shoring up shares. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jalil Hamid \par \uc2 594 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 1998\par 13:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR, Aug 18 (Reuters) - Malaysian Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad said on Tuesday the government was considering supporting the ailing stock market by buying shares and barring banks from selling stock. \par \par \uc2  Mahathir told reporters in the capital that money from a government-run fund for Moslem pilgrims might also be used to shore up the stock market, which slumped to a new 10-year low on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  He also said that the government was considering cutting taxes. \par \par \uc2  "It's quite difficult to raise taxes during these trying times. In fact we should lower taxes," he said. \par \par \uc2  Mahathir, who relishes verbal combat, earlier on Tuesday told a workers' rally that speculative attacks on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of emerging markets had reached a dangerous level and might prompt countries to retaliate. \par \par \uc2  He said, apparently in jest, that he feared attacks on the rouble might tempt Russia to use its nuclear weapons. \par \par \uc2  "The attacks by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders have reached a dangerous stage and are totally irresponsible," he said in a speech. \par \par \uc2  "I worry if the attacks continue on emerging markets, it may hit countries that have the capability to fight back. I worry, for example, if Russia continued to be pressured and they have nuclear bombs, then they may want to drop the bombs on those who attack them," he said, prompting laughter from some listeners. \par \par \uc2  "For us, we will continue to defend our country. We do not have atomic bombs or nuclear bombs. But I'm confident Malaysians can fend off all attacks mounted by any quarter." \par \par \uc2  Malaysia's stock market and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  have fallen sharply since the start of Asia's economic crisis in July 1997, and Mahathir has repeatedly blamed foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders. \par \par \uc2  Earlier on Tuesday, the Kuala Lumpur Stock Exchange's benchmark index, which has dropped by more than 70 percent since July 1997, fell to a new 10-year low of 312.90. \par \par \uc2  The index ended the day barely changed at 315.66 after Mahathir's comments. But the ringgit \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not impressed, closing the day slightly lower at 4.23 to a dollar against Monday's finish of 4.22. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said any moratorium on the sale of shares by banks could hammer bank stocks and in the long run lead to an increase in banks' sour loans. \par \par \uc2  The proposal would mean banks which are holding onto shares as loan collateral might no longer be able to force the sale of the shares if the loan was in default, said markets consultancy firm S&P's MMS International in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "If this is the case, it could trigger additional investor fright, as the move would essentially mean that corporate risk is now transferred to the banking sector, which may well cause the collapse of more banks under a mountain of 'uncollectible' bad debts," it said. \par \par \uc2  There were also questions about how the government planned to finance market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Mahathir said Malaysia and other countries whose \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been driven down by speculators had to take measures to protect their economies. \par \par \uc2  In Hong Kong, for example, the territorial government had to step into the market to buy shares but it was accused of interfering with free market, he said. \par \par \uc2  "You look at other countries, Hong Kong for example. There are some specific measures and of course they will get attacked by the foreign media and the others," he said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock and futures market on Friday to support sagging prices. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8i011n4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
