\par\par \b \uc2  ASIA MARKETS - Firm yen props regional  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 1,183 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 1998\par 11:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Aug 18 (Reuters) - Asian regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mostly firmer on Tuesday as the yen remained well capped at the 146 level on fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said earlier that Japan was always ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the yen. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen rose to a high of 146.44 but lost steam as traders were unwilling to go long on dollars above 146 yen. \par \par \uc2  But regional stocks were mostly weaker as support from Wall Street's overnight rally gave way to lingering worries over Russia's woes. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Russia's de facto devaluation of the rouble on Monday could put to the test China's commitment to maintain the value of the yuan and the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (HKMA) will to maintain the Hong Kong dollar peg. \par \par \uc2  NIKKEI REBOUND ON WALL STREET'S RALLY, SHRUG OFF RUSSIA \par \par \uc2  Japan's Nikkei index broke through the psychologically important 15,000 level on a technical rebound following Wall Street's overnight rally. \par \par \uc2  The key 225-share Nikkei average put on 1.82 percent to end at 15,063.79. \par \par \uc2  HANG SENG EASE BUT DOWNSIDE SEEN LIMITED \par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 , stocks slipped in cautious trade after Friday's unprecedented \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the HKMA. \par \par \uc2  The move was to punish speculators who had been playing off attacks on the local dollar to drive up interest rates and corresponding falls in the stock markets. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said continued short-covering and gains in Wall Street and the Nikkei helped avert a heavy selloff that many were expecting. \par \par \uc2  The HKMA declined to comment earlier on whether it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market again to fend off \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's provisional May-July unemployment rose to 4.8 percent from a revised 4.4 percent for the April-June period. \par \par \uc2  RINGGIT SLIPS IN LATE TRADE \par \par \uc2  The ringgit lost early gains and prospects for further upside looked poor as interest rates in Malaysia move even lower in a bid to stimulate the economy. \par \par \uc2  The National Economic Action Council said that recent cuts in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Negara's three-month \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate would allow commercial banks to cut their base lending rates. \par \par \uc2  The country's largest banks cut interest rates on Monday, following subdued inflation data and other indicators which pointed to a sharp slowdown in domestic demand. \par \par \uc2  The KLSE was little changed but looked sickly on the weak ringgit and uncertainty over the government's economic strategy. \par \par \uc2  Malaysian Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad said on Tuesday that the government was considering buying shares to support the ailing stock market. \par \par \uc2  Mahathir also told reporters the government was studying the possibility of following Taiwan's example and placing a moratorium on the sale of shares by banks. \par \par \uc2  KOSPI DIVES IN EARLY TRADE BUT WON KEEP FIRM \par \par \uc2  In Korea, the won was slightly firmer but the stock market dived around four percent in early business before recovering slightly. \par \par \uc2  Traders said it was just a case of Seoul playing catch-up because the market had already closed when news of the effective Russian devaluation hit the wires. \par \par \uc2  But there were also continued worries about the standoff at Hyundai's plant in Ulsan where workers and their families remained camped inside and thousands of riot police gathered outside. \par \par \uc2  Banking stocks were also pressuring the broader market as traders feared more capital reductions due to restructuring. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT UP ON IMPROVED SENTIMENT \par \par \uc2  The baht continued to strengthen with sentiment still being helped by the recent package to rescue ailing domestic banks. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the market was also keeping an eye on Thailand's fifth letter of intent with the IMF which is expected to be finalised soon and presented to the cabinet next week. \par \par \uc2  The payout of $450 million from the IMF sponsored $17.2 billion rescue package would be made available shortly after that. \par \par \uc2  But things were less rosy on the sovereign debt side. \par \par \uc2  Thailand said on Monday it would delay floating the first tranche of of its planned 5.0 billion dollars of government bonds. \par \par \uc2  Thai stocks fared better as investors bought back bank stocks after they were heavily sold in the previous session on concerns over Russia's problems. \par \par \uc2  RUPIAH SURGES AS RIOT FEARS FADE \par \par \uc2  The rupiah surged more than four percent in early trade as fears of rioting on Indoneisa's Independence on Monday faded. \par \par \uc2  The market was slightly bullish given the large amount of aid dollars due to flow into Indonesia in the coming months. \par \par \uc2  These dollars would be converted into rupiah for spending on infrastructure and other projects and dealers said that should support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But Jakarta stocks suffered as the rupiah rose. The fall in blue-chips including dual-listed Telkom and Indosat pulled down the main index. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the rupiah's strength made dual-listed shares relatively more expensive than their overseas counterpart causing arbitrage selling. \par \par \uc2  STI HITS FRESH 10-YEAR LOW \par \par \uc2  In Singapore, the local dollar and Straits Times Index registered losses on fears of speculative attacks on the local dollar. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was talk that hedge funds wanted to sell Singapore stocks and the Singapore dollar after Hong Kong made clear it would not tolerate any short selling there. \par \par \uc2      Change on the day TUE 18 0943GMT \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...Latest.....Prev Close...Pct Move \par \par \uc2   Ringgit....4.22.........4.23.........0.15% \par \par \uc2   Rupiah.....12300.00.....12900.00.....4.88% \par \par \uc2   Baht.......41.67........41.80........0.31% \par \par \uc2   Peso.......42.98........43.23........0.58% \par \par \uc2   Sing Dlr...1.77.........1.76........-0.48% \par \par \uc2   Taiwan Dlr.34.73........34.76........0.07% \par \par \uc2   Korean Won.1311.00......1332.00......1.60% \par \par \uc2   Change since start of Asia crisis \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...Latest.....July '97.....Pct Move \par \par \uc2   Ringgit....4.22.........2.52.......-40.34% \par \par \uc2   Rupiah.....12300.00.....2432.00....-80.23% \par \par \uc2   Baht.......41.66........25.90......-37.83% \par \par \uc2   Peso.......42.98........26.50......-38.34% \par \par \uc2   Sing Dlr...1.77.........1.43.......-19.14% \par \par \uc2   Taiwan Dlr.34.73........27.75......-20.11% \par \par \uc2   Korean Won.1311.00......842.00.....-35.77% \par \par \uc2  *************************************************** \par \par \uc2  STOCK MARKETS \par \par \uc2     Change on the day TUE 18 0943GMT \par \par \uc2  Market.....Current..Prev Close.Pct Move \par \par \uc2  Indonesia...403.86....414.63....-2.6% \par \p