\par\par \b \uc2  Hong Kong stocks open with moderate gains. \b0\par\par\uc2 634 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 1998\par 05:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Aug 18 (Reuters) - Hong Kong stocks opened moderately higher on Tuesday, with major blue chips holding on to the heavy gains made on Friday after the government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock and futures market. \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng Index rose 22.06 points, or 0.31 percent, at the opening to 7,246.75. \par \par \uc2  However, the blue chip index soon lost its gains to trade down five points, or 0.07 percent, at 7,219 in early trade. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said the market was cautiously watching to see whether speculators had backed off following the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock market on Friday and weighing the possibility of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The HKMA declined to comment on Tuesday whether it was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock market. \par \par \uc2  "This is going to be a choppy week. It is not over yet," said Howard Gorges, a director at South China Brokerage. \par \par \uc2  "Market movements will depend on what degree the speculators try to test the market by perhaps starting to short again and to what extent they feel they should start locking in profits. It will also depend on the extent the government is prepared to put a squeeze on them," he said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market pushed the Hang Seng Index up 8.47 percent on Friday as the government tried to fend off \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators. The market was closed on Monday for a public holiday. \par \par \uc2  A de facto devaluation of the Russian rouble on Monday added to concerns about the Hong Kong dollar, pushing interbank rates higher on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The overnight rate rose to 8.25-8.75 percent at the opening compared with a close of 7.50-8.00 percent on Friday and the benchmark three-month interbank rate rose to 11.10-11.62 percent compared with 10.50-11.00 percent. \par \par \uc2  "It is not a helpful factor to have in the background although Russia is a long way away," said Gorges. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said another reason to stay on the sidelines was unemployment data for May to July due at 1600 local time (0800 GMT). Economists said the jobless rate could rise to between 4.8 percent and 6.0 percent compared with the 15-year high of 4.5 percent in the April to June period. \par \par \uc2  "The unemployment rate is expected to be at 4.8 percent but the situation is deteriorating as we hear of layoffs almost every day," said Alan Pau, associate director at South China Securities. \par \par \uc2  He said trading was focused on several major blue chips with most of the buying seen in HSBC Holdings Plc . The stock, which went ex-dividend, was up HK$1.386, or 0.89 percent, at HK$156.50. \par \par \uc2  "There is possibly some short covering," said Geoff Galbraith, vice president institutional sales at DBS Securities. \par \par \uc2  "But I think the market should drift off because it was up too much on Friday and the fundamentals are still the same." \par \par \uc2  In the futures market, the August contract on the Hang Seng Index was up 10 points at 7,220 but the September contract fell 10 points to 7,240. \par \par \uc2  Among the major blue chips, Cheung Kong  rose HK$0.10 to HK$32.10 and Sun Hung Kai Properties  added HK$0.10 to HK$24.10. \par \par \uc2  But Hang Seng Bank  fell HK$0.60 to HK$39.40. \par \par \uc2  The red chip Hang Seng China-Affiliated Corporations Index was down 20 points, or 3.19 percent, at 596 and the H-share index lost nine points, or 3.25 percent, to 276. \par \par \uc2  Market turnover was HK$1.63 billion after the first hour of trade. \par \par \uc2  ((Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2843 6345; Fax (852) 2845 0636)) \par \par \uc2  ((Email: hongkong.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  *. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8i010uh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
