\par\par \b \uc2  HK stocks end slightly down, seeking direction. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kristina Zetterlund \par \uc2 371 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 1998\par 11:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Aug 18 (Reuters) - Hong Kong stocks closed Tuesday slightly lower with investors seeking direction after the dust settled from the government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock and futures market on Friday, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng Index dipped 13.77 points, or 0.19 percent, to finish at 7,210.92 after surging 8.47 percent on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "Most investors just don't know what to do at the moment because it is the first time the goverment has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market," said Ricky Tam, senior research analyst at Delta Asia Securities. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said the government may have been back in the market buying stocks on Tuesday, although less aggressively than on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The strategy is different today," said Glenn Lesko, head of sales trading at ABN-Amro Hoare Govett Asia Securities. \par \par \uc2  "They (the government) are not coming in to ramp it, they are only coming in to support it." \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment on Tuesday on whether it was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the securities and futures markets can only be considered and will only be considered when there is a corresponding 'play', if you like, on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  side," said \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  Financial Services Secretary Rafael Hui. \par \par \uc2  The government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday to fend off \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators. \par \par \uc2  Brokers expected the market to remain volatile in the near term. \par \par \uc2  "I think the market could actually climb higher in the days ahead," said Andrew Fernow, Vickers Ballas director of research. \par \par \uc2  "But then it may at certain levels be ripe for another selloff because of the tendendcy to take profits at higher levels, and also you are giving speculators another opportunity to establish short positions at higher levels." \par \par \uc2  Fernow believed the upside of the blue chip index was found at around 8,000, but added: \par \par \uc2  "We are still looking at the possibility of new lows being hit this year which would mean below 6,500." \par \par \uc2  Futures ended at a slight premium to the spot market. The August Hang Seng Index futures contract gained five points to 7,215 while the September contract was down 15 points at 7,235. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8i010ny\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
