\par\par \b \uc2  HK stocks set for fall after govt  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 468 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 1998\par 03:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Aug 18 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  stocks are expected on Tuesday to give back some of the government-induced gains made last Friday with investors viewing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the HKMA in the stock and futures markets negatively, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  With speculators still circling the Hong Kong dollar and the devaluation of the Russian rouble heightening jitters about the stability of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Hang Seng Index may get little help from overnight gains on Wall Street, they said. \par \par \uc2  "The government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a bad thing that will dampen investor interest in Hong Kong because it adds policy risk to the market," said Alex Wong, research manager at OSK Asia Securities. \par \par \uc2  "Because the market is so volatile, investors will be facing big risks at the present level and in the present situation." \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng Index soared 8.47 percent to close at 7,224.69 points on Friday as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock and futures market to fend off \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators. \par \par \uc2  The market was closed on Monday for a public holiday. \par \par \uc2  "I think initially we will see a pullback but because the market is still worried about further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the downside will not be too much," said Wong. \par \par \uc2  He expected a range of 6,900 to 7,400 points. \par \par \uc2  Monday's de facto devaluation of the Russian rouble would keep speculative pressures on the Hong Kong dollar, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  "The problem here is the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so the more emerging markets undergo devaluations, the more pressure there will be on Hong Kong," said Kent Rossiter, institutional sales manager at Nikko Securities. \par \par \uc2  Unemployment data due out later on Tuesday would be another negative, he said. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong government is set to release jobless figures for the May to July period at 1600 local time (0800 GMT) and economists are expecting the rate to reach up to 6.0 percent from a 15-year high of 4.5 percent for the April to June period. \par \par \uc2  "There will be a strong temptation for locals and international institutions to take profits from Friday's rally," said Douglas Hansen-Luke, associate director at South China Securities. \par \par \uc2  However a gain of 149.85 points, or 1.78 percent, in the Dow Industrials overnight to 8,574.85 points and a rise of 264 points, or 1.78 percent, in Japan's Nikkei 225 to 15,058 on Tuesday morning, would help limit the downside, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  "I am not predicting gloom and doom but I would not be suprised for the market to come off," said Rossiter. \par \par \uc2  He expected the Hang Seng Index to trade around 7,000 points. \par \par \uc2  ((Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2843 6345; Fax (852) 2845 0636)) \par \par \uc2  ((Email: hongkong.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  *. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8i010mb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
