\par\par \b \uc2  HK stocks end morning weak but losses limited. \b0\par\par\uc2 805 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 1998\par 07:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Aug 18 (Reuters) - Hong Kong stocks ended the morning weak on Tuesday as analysts viewed the hefty gains sparked by government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday as unsustainable amid \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weak economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  However, last week's stock and futures buying by the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  prompted continued short-covering, while gains on Wall Street and in Tokyo also helped to avert the heavy selloff some were expecting on Tuesday, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng Index closed the morning down 19.23 points, or 0.27 percent, at 7,205.46 points after rallying 8.47 percent on Friday. Turnover was HK$2.96 billion compared with HK$4.80 billion at Friday's midsession. \par \par \uc2  "There are so many variables now at play so the market is in a state of confusion," said Robert Sassoon, head of research at SG Securities. \par \par \uc2  "I don't know whether the government is still continuing to purchase although they have certainly indicated that they will continue, but in the long run it should not have any impact at all. Market forces always prevail in the end," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment on Tuesday on whether it was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market after it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday in the stock and futures market to fend off \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators. \par \par \uc2  "It will be up to the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  to decide what will be the appropriate action for today, if any," \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  Financial Services Secretary Rafael Hui said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the securities and futures markets can only be considered and will only be considered when there is a corresponding 'play', if you like, on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  side." \par \par \uc2  Steven Thompson, chief analyst at Nikko Research Center, said the government's actions on Friday smacked of desperation. \par \par \uc2  Once the short-sellers had unwound some of their positions in the futures market, they would rebuild them, he said. \par \par \uc2  "We have had a gradual accumulation of these shorts. If they continue to unwind, they will build new positions again from a much lower base so this will put even more downward pressure on the index," said Thompson. \par \par \uc2  In the futures market, the August contract on the Hang Seng Index was down 35 points at 7,175 and the September contract fell 40 points to 7,210. \par \par \uc2  Thompson said the Hang Seng Index could still resume its sharp downtrend, possibly testing 6,000 points. \par \par \uc2  "Right now we think the economy is contracting by about six percent. It is a very steep recession relative to our recent history and we should be taking into account that fundamental fact," he said. \par \par \uc2  More bad news on the economy was expected on Tuesday with the government set to release May to July unemployment statistics at 1600 local time (0800 GMT). \par \par \uc2  Economists expect the jobless rate to rise to between 4.8 percent and 6.0 percent compared with a rate of 4.5 percent in the April to June period. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the impact of Monday's devaluation of the Russian rouble had been limited with a public holiday in Hong Kong on Monday allowing investors to digest the news. \par \par \uc2  "Perhaps it was fortunate that we had a day to think about it before opening. If we had been open yesterday it might have had some effect," said Thompson. \par \par \uc2  However, the devaluation pushed local interbank rates higher. The overnight rate was at 8.50-8.62 percent compared with a close of 7.50-8.00 on Friday and the three month interbank rate was at 11.50-12.00 percent compared with 10.50-11.00 percent on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said gains on Wall Street and in Tokyo helped limit stock market losses on Tuesday. The Dow industrials rose 149.85 points, or 1.78 percent, to close at 8,574.85 overnight while in Tokyo, the Nikkei 225 was up 230 points or 1.55 percent, at 15,025. \par \par \uc2  Shares in HSBC Holdings Plc  rose HK$0.886, or 0.57 percent, to HK$156.00 but subsidiary Hang Seng Bank Ltd  fell HK$1.20, or 3.00 percent, to HK$38.80. \par \par \uc2  In the property sector, Cheung Kong  gained HK$0.10 to HK$32.10 and Sun Hung Kai Properties Ltd  added HK$0.10 to HK$24.10. \par \par \uc2  China plays fell sharply, hurt by devastating floods in China and the devaluation of the Russian rouble. \par \par \uc2  The red chip Hang Seng China-Affiliated Corporations Index ended the morning down 33.18 points, or 5.39 percent, at 582.39 and the H-share index tumbled 15.93 points, or 5.58 percent, at 269.53. \par \par \uc2  ((Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2843 6345; Fax (852) 2845 0636)) \par \par \uc2  ((Email: hongkong.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  *. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8i0100t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
