\par\par \b \uc2  Hong Kong ready to lock horns with speculators. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tan Ee Lyn \par \uc2 726 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 1998\par 10:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Aug 17 (Reuters) - Hong Kong, sagging under a host of economic woes, is poised for a long, drawn-out battle with international speculators to protect the local dollar and its 15-year-old linked exchange-rate system. \par \par \uc2  Joseph Yam, head of Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA), said on Monday the territory was willing to lock horns with the international players who have attacked Hong Kong markets. \par \par \uc2  In a letter to London's Financial Times newspaper, he said the government would step back from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock and futures markets only when speculative manipulation of the Hong Kong dollar stopped. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar has been pegged at HK$7.8 to the U.S. dollar since 1983. \par \par \uc2  "Meanwhile we shall act to deter \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulators," he wrote. "We do object to people manipulating our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to engineer extreme conditions in the interbank market and high interest rates to make profits in the short positions in stock index futures." \par \par \uc2  The government took the unprecedented step on Friday of buying local stocks and futures to hit back at speculators, whom it accused of trying to break the Hong Kong dollar peg. \par \par \uc2  After a day of government buying, the blue chip Hang Seng Index closed up 8.47 percent on Friday at 7,224.69 points. It had been at five-year lows during the week. \par \par \uc2  Yam and other officials said the aim of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was to punish speculators who had been playing off attacks on the local dollar to drive up interest rates and corresponding falls in the stock market. \par \par \uc2  Market players who had short-sold the Hong Kong stock market - that is, borrowed stock and sold it in anticipation of later buying it back cheaper - may have lost heavily when the government drove prices higher. \par \par \uc2  The government move provoked some applause but also a great deal of criticism by those who saw it as an unjustifiable break in a long-standing free-market policy. \par \par \uc2  An editorial on Monday in the respected Hong Kong Economic Journal questioned the government's move. \par \par \uc2  "Speculators are using a range of tactics to profit from Hong Kong's problems. Whatever measure the Hong Kong government takes, whether it is to buy the Hong Kong dollar or buy Hong Kong stocks, will only hurt some of the speculators, but others will profit," the newspaper said. \par \par \uc2  By amassing stocks and futures during such volatile times the government would only put Hong Kong reserves at risk, it added. \par \par \uc2  "The government, having bought so many stocks and futures, could make itself more vulnerable given the volatility in the stock and futures market." \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong had US$96.5 billion in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves at end June. Officials have declined to say how much was spent in the last week protecting the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  And the question now is how long Hong Kong can keep up the battle, the Hong Kong Standard newspaper said in an editorial. \par \par \uc2  "When the market opens tomorrow, the speculators might be back in full force," it said. "Then more of our reserves will have to be commandeered to keep them at bay. Whether we can last the distance is anybody's guess." \par \par \uc2  It added Hong Kong had no idea how much money speculators could bring to bear in the coming attacks but the speculators knew exactly how much money \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  had for a dollar defence. \par \par \uc2  The government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  surprised many people in the former British colony of 6.6 million, which returned to Chinese rule last year. \par \par \uc2  Shortly after Britain returned the territory to Beijing after more than 150 years of colonial rule, the new special administrative region of China was swept up in the regional economic crisis. \par \par \uc2  The overall economy shrank 2.8 percent in the first quarter of 1998 from a year earlier. Unemployment, already at 15-year highs, is expected to rise further when new figures are issued later this week. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong residents, accustomed to years of healthy growth, are hurting. About 200 Christians joined a cruise down Victoria Harbour on Sunday to pray for divine \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  With hands held up in the air, the Christians prayed for Hong Kong leader Tung Chee-hwa to embrace their God to save the territory's battered economy. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8h037zb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
