\par\par \b \uc2  Government  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  May Harm  \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong \highlight\uc2 's International Image \b0\par\par\uc2  By G. Bruce Knecht and Erik Guyot, Staff Reporters of The Wall Street Journal \par \uc2 1,251 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 1998\par 06:53\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- Stepping away from a longstanding commitment to unhindered markets, monetary authorities undertook a major and unprecedented \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stock and futures markets Friday to hurt speculators who are trying to force a devaluation of the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  While the move forced an 8.47% jump in the benchmark Hang Seng stock index, which closed at 7224.69 Friday, some big investors said it would do far more harm than good, denting the city's reputation as a free-market haven and raising unsustainable expectations the government will prop up the market if it comes under more severe pressure. Those perceptions could harm Hong Kong's role as an international financial center. \par \par \uc2  The city's top officials insisted the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was undertaken only to deter a particular form of speculation rather than to bolster the stock market. "The government has no intention to artificially support the stock market," the city's chief executive, Tung Chee-hwa, said. "The government will, as before, adhere strictly to our longstanding policy of nonintervention in the stock and futures markets." \par \par \uc2  At the same time, Mr. Tung said he would take "resolute action to reduce market distortion." His finance secretary, Donald Tsang, accused speculators of attacking Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a "whole host of improper measures," including the spreading of "vicious rumors" about a devaluation of Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the stability of its banks. \par \par \uc2  Government officials declined to say whether there would be more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week or to reveal how much was spent in Friday's buying, which came at the end of a week that saw the Hang Seng index sink to a five-year low, almost two-thirds off its peak of a year ago. Traders estimated that the government spent between 2 billion and 3 billion Hong Kong dollars (US$258 million and US$387 million) acquiring shares in the 33 companies that compose the index as well as an undetermined amount on stock-index futures, accounting for as much as a third of the turnover Friday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock market was aimed at catching \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators where they profit in staging attacks on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Messrs. Tung and Tsang said speculators took advantage of the fact that maintaining the value of the Hong Kong dollar requires the monetary authorities to increase high interest rates, which in turn decrease stock-market prices. They said speculators have entered both \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets to attack the Hong Kong dollar in order to push up local interest rates and profit from the resulting fall in share prices. The moves feed an exodus from Hong Kong-dollar investments and put additional pressure on the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's dollar has been linked to the U.S. dollar since 1983 at a rate of about 7.8 to the U.S. dollar. It is the only freely traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia that hasn't been devalued in the last year, and speculation against the "pet" has soared in recent months. Expectations also have risen that China, which has sovereignty over Hong Kong, might feel pressure to devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan. (\b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials said they informed Beijing of their decision to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock market only after they had decided to do it.) \par \par \uc2  Authorities increased the risk of speculating to make speculators more cautious, said Donald Hanna, an economist at Goldman, Sachs & Co. By making "speculative profits more uncertain," he said, the move should "reduce the number of attacks." \par \par \uc2  The danger of the government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday, market participants said, is that the move could take on a momentum of its own or be viewed by investors as presaging a generally more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  approach. "Even if the government believes it's taking an extraordinary measure, we're going into extraordinary times, and that might mean they have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  every month," said Marc Faber, a \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  money manager. \par \par \uc2  "The government is launching a battle against the marketplace, but eventually the market will prevail," Mr. Faber added. Worse still, he contends that any form of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could postpone \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  recovery by artificially supporting stock prices. "Like in Japan, which has supported markets for a long time," he said, "this will prolong the agony." \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities began \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Tokyo's stock market in October 1990, when the country's Nikkei stock average had fallen by half in a single year. At first, as in Hong Kong, the index responded positively, skyrocketing 13% in a single day. That success, however, launched the government into an extended, on-again-off-again game of trying to prop up the stock market. That ultimately entailed huge costs, undermining the country's credibility in carrying out its professed goal of freeing Tokyo's capital markets from overweening regulation, and it wasn't particularly successful. \par \par \uc2  Less than two years after the first major manipulation, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  took the game to a new level by arm-twisting major insurers into buying as the Nikkei fell through 18000. Three times since then, the market has dropped to the mid-14000 level. \par \par \uc2  Colin Bradbury, head of sales at Jardine Fleming Securities Ltd., said that in Hong Kong, the authorities "don't understand that for international investors the one thing Hong Kong has going for it is its laissez faire system." \par \par \uc2  Moreover, by not allowing asset prices to move freely, the government is hampering the function of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board -- the system that links the Hong Kong dollar to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- putting a question mark over its political commitment to hold the peg no matter what the economic cost. Government officials have been aware of those concerns. In recent weeks, officials have solicited views from foreign investors "as to how far they can go in interfering in the market," Mr. Bradbury said. \par \par \uc2  The success of the government's move won't be immediately clear. The stock market is closed Monday for a holiday, although \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders will be able to deal in Singapore and elsewhere. \par \par \uc2  The political impact was also unclear over the weekend. "It generates a sense that government can fight back -- that it's not a one-way street," Goldman's Mr. Hanna said. \par \par \uc2  But Albert Ho, spokesman for the Democratic Party, Hong Kong's largest political party, said it was "unwise" and "unrealistic" for the government to try to drive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators out of financial markets. "Hong Kong is a free market, and speculation is a part of life in Hong Kong," Mr. Ho said. \par \par \uc2  "We are fully aware that we are undergoing a very long adjustment period and adjustment will be painful," said Mr. Tung, the city's chief executive, "but we are well prepared as a community to take such pain." \par \par \uc2  In June, the government froze new land sales in a bid to bolster slumping property prices, and in the 1980s the government bailed out several banks and allowed the city's futures market to suspend \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the 1987 global stock-market crash. \par \par \uc2  But with Friday's intevention, Hong Kong "has started down a dead-end road of interference in the market," warned the Chinese language Hong Kong Economic Journal in an editorial over the weekend. \par \par \uc2  --- \par \par \uc2  Henny Sender and Bill Spindle contributed to this article. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du8h016bu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
