\par\par \b \uc2  Tung says HK gov't moved to foil speculators. \b0\par\par\uc2 325 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 1998\par 13:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Aug 14 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  leader Tung Chee-hwa said his administration \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock and futures markets on Friday to foil speculators and not to support prices. \par \par \uc2  "The government has no intention to artificially support the stock market. The measures we have taken today are directed against specifically speculative activities," he told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Tung said: "We are fully aware that we are going through a very long adjustment period and the adjustment will be painful." \par \par \uc2  He said a healthy environment was required for our economy "to undergo effective and painful adjustment...to do so, we must have a stable interest rate environment". \par \par \uc2  Tung said his administration had been keeping an eye on speculators. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday came after careful and detailed consideration. \par \par \uc2  "There has been massive speculative activities in the Hong Kong money market and the stock futures market," he said. \par \par \uc2  Tung said Hong Kong authorities worked out their moves themselves but "obviously the central (Beijing) government knows about it". \par \par \uc2  "It is difficult to say whether speculative activities will continue but we will watch carefully every time and take decisive measures if needed," he added. \par \par \uc2  "We are doing it purely against speculative play which is linked to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  play, and the stock and futures market, and we will do it time and time again in order to make our point," he said. \par \par \uc2  Joseph Yam, head of the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , told reporters at a separate briefing that it was the first time the government had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the securities market. \par \par \uc2  He vowed to leave "no stone unturned" in the defence of the Hong Kong-U.S. dollar peg. \par \par \uc2  Yam said the HKMA would hold the stocks it purchased on Friday until speculators had left and then sell the stocks gradually. He added that the speculators are "still out there". \par \par \uc2  - Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2843 6441. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8e036fo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
