\par\par \b \uc2  Hong Kong markets shrug off HK dlr options rumours. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrea Ricci \par \uc2 837 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 1998\par 12:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Aug 14 (Reuters) - The Hong Kong dollar was rock solid and forward rates were sharply lower on Friday despite talk of potentially market-destabilising options contracts expiring next week, the first on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "It doesn't look like there is going to be an attack on the Hong Kong dollar during the weekend. Activities were mainly focused on position-squaring today," a dealer at a U.S. commercial bank said at the close of trade. \par \par \uc2  For weeks, traders have been saying there was a pile of derivative contracts related to the Hong Kong dollar expiring in August that could rattle local markets. \par \par \uc2  The most persistent - and decidedly unconfirmed - rumour was that there was an enormous call option on the Hong Kong dollar with an exercise price of HK$7.8 per US$1. \par \par \uc2  "The talk is that there is a knockout option maturing on Tuesday," said an analyst at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  "If the Hong Kong dollar fails to touch 7.8, the people that bought the option get paid a big return. If it touches 7.8, then the person that issued the option gets all the money," in the form of the option premium, he said. "That's the rumour." \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar was linked to the U.S. dollar at a rate of HK$7.8 per US$1 in 1983. It is permitted to trade in a narrow band around that level and typically trades on the strong side. \par \par \uc2  In fact, the Hong Kong dollar spot rate hasn't flirted with 7.8 since 1991 and hasn't weakened past 7.75 since mid-1993. \par \par \uc2  It was at 7.7490/00 at Friday's close. \par \par \uc2  The 7.75 level is widely seen as the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA), which often is noted buying modest amounts of Hong Kong dollars at that level. \par \par \uc2  If there is a call option on the 7.8, it would imply that the buyer of the option strongly believed that the HKMA would defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while the seller believed the opposite. \par \par \uc2  Traders pointed out that the peg needn't break for the option to expire without being exercised. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, by writing an option at 7.8, the seller was more likely betting that the HKMA could not hold the rate at the super support level of 7.75 - which would spark heavy sales, pushing the local dollar to 7.8, where there would be intense pressure through the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system to keep it at 7.8. \par \par \uc2  But few really believe that whatever options have been put on the Hong Kong dollar are that simple. More likely, they say, the parties involved also have their hands in other transactions, perhaps options on Hong Kong stock futures. \par \par \uc2  Traders said speculators win as long as markets fluctuate. \par \par \uc2  "There are a lot of rumours that hedge funds are \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to scare people off. We know the hedge funds lose money if the markets don't go crazy, and they are starting to launch rumours to cash in on their positions," said an interest rate derivatives trader at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  Financial Secretary Donald Tsang on Friday said the HKMA had used the Exchange Fund to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  stock and futures markets to foil speculators, a highly unusual move. He said that while the government had not abandoned its policy of non-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it would act in the markets again under exceptional circumstances. \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng Index surged 8.47 percent, closing at 7,224.69 \par \par \uc2  Tsang blasted speculators for hurting the public, saying their aim was to make quick profits through the futures market. \par \par \uc2  Conventional wisdom is that speculators are not really targeting the Hong Kong dollar when they sell it. \par \par \uc2  Rather, they are trying to push up rates and cause stock prices to fall, either because they already have shorted the share market or plan to buy stocks at bargain prices. \par \par \uc2  Last week, overseas investment banks and hedge funds launched a massive attack on the Hong Kong dollar, selling several billion U.S. dollars worth of Hong Kong dollars in the spot and forward markets. \par \par \uc2  Under Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, pressures for a weak Hong Kong dollar automatically cause rates to rise. \par \par \uc2  Interest rates rose in response to the attack, but by only a few hundred basis points, far less than in October 1997, when an assault on the local dollar of comparable size caused the overnight rate to soar to 300 percent. \par \par \uc2  The rate rise was checked because the HKMA bought Hong Kong dollars for the government treasury, not through the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board, so the dollars were recirculated in the banking system. \par \par \uc2  The attack rattled Hong Kong shares, but dealers said the losses would have been much greater if rates had surged. \par \par \uc2  - Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2843-6371; Fax (852) 2845-0636 \par \par \uc2  - Email: hongkong.newsroom@reuters.com. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8e036ad\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
