\par\par \b \uc2  Hong Kong stocks jump after government steps in. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kristina Zetterlund \par \uc2 409 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 1998\par 16:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Reuters) - Hong Kong stocks soared Friday as the territory's goverment stepped into the financial markets in a bid to foil speculators. \par \par \uc2  The main Hang Seng Index jumped 564.27 points, or 8.5 percent, to 7,224.69. It was the ninth biggest point gain ever for the blue-chip index, which has slumped in line with the severe economic downturn afflicting Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  "The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been buying up the Hong Kong dollars by selling U.S. dollars in the market," Hong Kong Financial Secretary Donald Tsang said after the market closed. \par \par \uc2  The authority also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock and futures market Friday to foil speculators, investors said. \par \par \uc2  Tsang said that hedge funds, private partnerships that often make speculative investments, had been selling Hong Kong dollars recently in a bid to push down stocks and profit from positions they had taken, in effect betting a drop would occur. \par \par \uc2  "This would enable them to benefit from their very substantial short positions which they had accumulated in the Hang Seng Index futures market," Tsang said. Short selling is selling borrowed stock in a bid to profit by buying it back later at a lower price. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said the market also got support from a firmer Japanese yen. \par \par \uc2  But brokers doubted whether the gains were sustainable given continued worries about the stability of the yen and the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan. \par \par \uc2  "We are not out of the woods yet, but probably we will see some support when the market moves below 7,000," said Alex Tang, head of research at Yamaichi International. \par \par \uc2  Tang expected the market to trade in a range of 7,000 to 8,000 in the medium term. \par \par \uc2  "The market fall has been exaggerated because the hedge fund managers have been selling the equity market," said David Williamson, director at Indosuez W.I. Carr. \par \par \uc2  Tsang declined to detail what actions authorities took but said the government would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again if the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar came under repeated attack. He said the government had ample reserves to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has been pegged at HK$7.80 to the U.S. dollar since 1983. \par \par \uc2  "We have total resolve and we are going through all motions to defend the linked exchange," he added. \par \par \uc2  Investors also eyed the Japanese yen as the U.S. dollar slipped below 145 yen. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8e03663\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
