\par\par \b \uc2  London HK stocks steady in active two-way trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 382 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 1998\par 17:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 14 (Reuters) - Hong Kong stocks in London were little changed on Friday but dealers reported intense two-way trading in the blue chips ahead of a market holiday on Monday. \par \par \uc2  London traders were little impressed by an 8.5 percent rebound in the Hang Seng index, which was seen as the result of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock market by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  government rather than a change in sentiment. \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng London reference index was only 3.98 points ahead at 7,228.67. \par \par \uc2  "The index doesn't reflect the level of activity as it has been very busy across the board, probably the busiest day for the past three months, with two-way business in all the leaders," one trader said. \par \par \uc2  Another noted that the market had started weaker amid fears the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets would take their revenge against the Hong Kong government knocking prices lower. \par \par \uc2  A rebound in global markets prompted a recovery in the afternoon. "We don't think this (Hong Kong) rally is sustainable and with the market shut on Monday nobody wanted to take big positions," the second dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Index heavyweight HSBC Holdings   was among the most active stocks and ended with a gain of HK$2.5 to HK$160.5, dragged higher mainly by a strong performance of the shares on the London Stock Exchange. \par \par \uc2  Other active issues included New World Development , which eased HK$0.75 to HK$8.775, and Hutchison Whampoa, up HK$0.15 from its Hong Kong close. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, the Hang Seng index jumped 564.27 points to 7,224.68. The August future contract added 600 points to 7,210. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong Financial Secretary Donald Tsang announced he had asked the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) to launch "counter activities in the stocks and futures market" against speculators who had attacked the Hong Kong dollar in order to influence stock prices. \par \par \uc2  Officials refused to disclose how much the HKMA spent buying shares and futures contract, but they insisted their aim was to foil speculators, not prop up the sagging market. \par \par \uc2  ((London newsroom +44 171 542 8712 fax +44 171 583 3769, uk.stocks.news@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8e034m7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
