\par\par \b \uc2  H.K. stocks soar, most other Asian markets higher. \b0\par\par\uc2 527 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 1998\par 17:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE (Reuters) - Stocks soared in Hong Kong on Friday after the government stepped into financial markets in a bid to foil speculators, while most other Asian markets also posted gains. \par \par \uc2  The yen recovered some ground against the dollar, helping to offset some of the concerns about Russia's growing financial problems. Stocks in Thailand jumped 5.5 percent and share prices also rose in Indonesia, the Philippines, South Korea and Singapore. But stocks fell in Japan. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong stocks led the rally, with the main Hang Seng Index jumping 564.27 points, or 8.5 percent, to 7,224.69. It was the ninth-biggest point gain for the blue chip index, which has slumped amid the territory's recession. \par \par \uc2  "The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been buying up the Hong Kong dollars by selling U.S. dollars in the market," Hong Kong Financial Secretary Donald Tsang said after the market closed. The authority also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock and futures market Friday to foil speculators, investors said. \par \par \uc2  Tsang said that hedge funds, private partnerships that often make speculative investments, had been selling Hong Kong dollars recently in a bid to push down stocks and profit from positions they had taken in effect betting a drop would occur. \par \par \uc2  Tsang declined to detail what actions authorities took but said the government would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again if the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar came under repeated attack. He said the government had ample reserves to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has been pegged at HK$7.80 to the U.S. dollar since 1983. \par \par \uc2  "We have total resolve and we are going through all motions to defend the linked exchange," he added. \par \par \uc2  In Tokyo, the Nikkei average fell 258.09 points, or 1.7 percent, to 15,123.93. on lingering worries about Japan's banking sector and an overnight drop in U.S. stocks that sparked fears of a global stock sell-off, traders said. \par \par \uc2  But the stronger yen and a recovery in other Asian stock prices lent some support, they said. \par \par \uc2  The yen recovered to about 144 against the dollar in Asian trading, up from about 145 late in New York on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, Thai stocks rose, supported by a bank reform package announced by the government. Under the plan, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is effectively going to nationalise the worst performing institutions via debt for equity swaps. \par \par \uc2  The main Set index rose 12.75 points to 243.62. \par \par \uc2  In Indonesia, the Jakarta composite index rose 11.69 points, or 2.9 percent, to 414.63, helped by the strong rise in Hong Kong, traders and analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "Regional stock trading is a dominant factor in the trading performance of the Jakarta market today," one analyst said. \par \par \uc2  In the Philippines, Manila's composite index rose 34.63, or 2.7 percent, to close at 1,337.92. \par \par \uc2  South Korea's Kospi index rose 1.6 points, or 0.5 percent, to 304.61, restrained by lingering worries about the outlook for Russia, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's Straits Times index added 8.37, or 0.9 percent, to 974.11. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8e033tw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
