\par\par \b \uc2  WEEKAHEAD-Hong Kong stocks still dogged by  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Karen Richardson \par \uc2 661 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 1998\par 17:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Aug 16 (Reuters) - Hong Kong's grim fundamentals and persistent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears could cap any gains in the stock market this coming week and could even lead to a fall despite government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week to fend off speculators. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock and stock futures markets last Friday amid speculation against the Hong Kong dollar, fuelling a 564.27 point, or 8.47 percent, rally in the Hang Seng Index to 7,224.69. \par \par \uc2  Over the week the blue chip index gained 206.28 points, or 2.9 percent after touching five-year lows during the week. The market will be closed on Monday for a public holiday. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's Chief Executive Tung Chee-hwa said on Friday the government had no intention of artificially supporting the stock market, but \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  only to foil speculators. \par \par \uc2  "It won't change a thing. I think they (the speculators) will come back with a vengeance," said Steven Thompson, chief analyst at Nikko Research Center. \par \par \uc2  He said the Hang Seng Index would slide to a low of 6,000 points within the next two to three weeks. \par \par \uc2  "They may have achieved a certain goal of burning these speculators, but at the same time they have probably damaged Hong Kong's reputation," said Thompson, adding some legitimate buyers may have been hurt by the government's decision. \par \par \uc2  The HKMA also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Friday, by selling treasury bills to buy the local dollar, and recirculating those dollars back into the banking system. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could lessen confidence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar peg to the U.S. dollar, since the government had been forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "With the HKMA coming in and buying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...even if they are committed to the peg, it raises the spectre that maybe they are not. And then the whole thing starts to feed upon itself," said Robert Rountree, head of research at Prudential-Bache Securities. \par \par \uc2  "As long as speculators and hedge funds feel the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  or the authorities are anything less than totally committed to the peg, then they can push the market down as low as they want," Rountree said. \par \par \uc2  Alex Wong, research manager at OSK Asia Securities, said next week's upside would be limited, with the first resistance at 7,350 points and then at 7,600. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have only a short-term impact on the market, because the fundamentals are still unchanged. Interest rates are high and the yen is still weak," he said. \par \par \uc2  Wong said some pullback could occur as the market tested the level for another \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "But (the HKMA) can't keep doing this, because it has limited resources," he added. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the yen's volatility would continue to put pressure on the Chinese yuan, which would weigh on sentiment toward the Hong Kong dollar peg. \par \par \uc2  "We need to know when the political leaders of Southeast Asian and northeast Asian economies are going to get their acts together," said Thompson. \par \par \uc2  "If the Japanese got their act together and sorted out their banking system promptly...we could be on the road to recovery." \par \par \uc2  Thompson said Hong Kong's gross domestic product contraction of 2.8 percent for the first quarter of 1998 was a major factor in the index's long-term downtrend. \par \par \uc2  "That obviously means all our estimates have to be revised down, causing pressure on the Hang Seng Index," he said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said unemployment figures for May to July, due on Tuesday, would dampen sentiment. Economists expect the jobless rate to rise to between 4.8 percent to six percent from 4.5 percent in the three months to June. \par \par \uc2  More anaemic corporate results would also weigh on the market, analysts said. Cheung Kong Infrastructure Holdings Ltd  is due to release interim results on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8e033q6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
