\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Profit, not peg, is target in HK$ attacks. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Phil Smith \par \uc2 1,074 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 1998\par 09:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Aug 14 (Reuters) - There has been much talk of "speculative" attacks on the Hong Kong dollar in the past year but there is more to these assaults than a single-minded attempt by the market to break the peg. \par \par \uc2  At times since the start of the Asia crisis pressure has developed on the Hong Kong dollar because it is basically the last pegged \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia and the Hong Kong economy has suffered pretty much equally with the rest of the region. \par \par \uc2  When pressure develops on the spot rate, for whatever reason, many other traders pile in to trade the Hang Seng and Hong Kong dollar forwards because there is money to be made. \par \par \uc2  "It's a situation where it's quite easy to make money with little risk," said one Singapore based trader. "As long as you can get into the forwards at a reasonable rate you can make a good turn because when the (interest) rates start to go (up) they really go." \par \par \uc2  The situation develops because of the way Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board works and the dramatic impact that downward pressure on the HK dollar has on interest rates and therefore the forward market premiums. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , linked to the U.S. dollar since 1983 after a speculative attack, is the last tradable pegged \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region. It is pegged at HK$7.8 per US$1 and is permitted to float in a narrow band around that level. \par \par \uc2  Under the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, note-issuing banks buy or sell Hong Kong dollars to the public but they are bound to undertake a reverse transaction by selling or buying Hong Kong dollars from the territory's Exchange Fund. \par \par \uc2  This drains, or injects, liquidity into the monetary system so downward pressure on the strong Hong Kong dollar will raise interest rates and vice-versa. \par \par \uc2  It is this - the fact that interest rates jump when there is pressure on the spot rate - that creates a situation where forward purchases of U.S. dollars can become extremely profitable. \par \par \uc2  An operator, anticipating pressure on the spot Hong Kong dollar would buy forward U.S. dollars, effectively going short of Hong Kong dollars forward. \par \par \uc2  The same operator would then short the Hang Seng, most probably on the futures market, because the stock market is almost bound to fall as interest rates surge. \par \par \uc2  By then selling spot Hong Kong dollars, along with the rest of the market, the player would see the forward premium on his U.S. dollars kick up as interest rates jumped. \par \par \uc2  The added bonus of being short of the stock market would boost his profits when the Hang Seng fell out of bed on the higher interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The long forward dollar position means the short Hong Kong dollar spot is effectively funded because money is made on the forward side. \par \par \uc2  The situation is unlike speculative attacks on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as sterling in 1992 where traders were looking to make money purely on the extent of the fall if a devaluation is forced - which they eventually did, to the tune of 15 percent. \par \par \uc2  The risk of a substantial loss on the short spot position is also much less with the Hong Kong dollar than with more freely floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  This is because its upside is as limited by the peg system as its downside. Therefore, even if you are short of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars at the time of heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stave off a speculative attack, any loss on the upside is extremely limited. \par \par \uc2  "What makes the Hong Kong possible to play in this manner is the fact that it's pegged," said Chiang Yao Chye, head of Asia Pacific research at CIBC in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "So even if the authorities defend the Hong Kong dollar they are not going to try and make it appreciate. If the spot appreciates you're obviously looking at losses on your spot position." \par \par \uc2  The economic cost of maintaining the peg can be high and the money the market is making with these forward plays is basically draining out of the Hong Kong asset markets. \par \par \uc2  The main cost of maintaining the peg is the hit the asset markets receive when interest rates rise as the peg comes under pressure. In Hong Kong the property market has suffered badly of late and the authorities have moved to shore up prices. \par \par \uc2  But analysts say that to maintain a credible commitment to the peg, authorities should be self-disciplined enough to allow asset markets to fall and take the strain. It is simply one of the costs of maintaining the peg. \par \par \uc2  By moving to help the property market the government is effectively showing that it does not like the impact the peg is having on the economy. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) also recently borrowed and bought Hong Kong dollars for the government treasury. \par \par \uc2  Because these dollars will flow back into the system as they are used to offset a fiscal deficit, the action by the HKMA is tantamount to sterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  so the net impact on liquidity over time is zero. \par \par \uc2  "It seems to me that maybe the HKMA does not like the side-effects of maintaining the peg and some of these moves we've seen recently show that perhaps they are getting soft and unwilling to take it on the chin like they have in the past," said one Singapore analyst who preferred not to be named. \par \par \uc2  If these kinds of attacks on the Hong Kong peg continue, it heightens the chance that the peg will eventually break, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  But a Reuters poll this week showed that seven out of 10 economists believed the Hong Kong dollar would stay linked to its U.S. counterpart, with no likelihood of a future de-pegging. \par \par \uc2  But some have their doubts, with one thinking the peg will hold out until mid-1999, while another said the risks were high that the peg would break over the next six to 12 months. \par \par \uc2  "There is no reason why people think there will always be pressure on the Hong Kong dollar," CIBC's Chiang said. \par \par \uc2  "But if things are not going well it's an excuse and if enough people jump on the bandwagon and interest rates go high enough you make a profit anyway, whether the peg breaks or not." \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8e0336w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
