\par\par \b \uc2  HKMA  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Leads Some To Question Peg Commitment \b0\par\par\uc2  By Adam S. Najberg \par \uc2 335 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 1998\par 11:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent days has worried some analysts, who say it will slow the territory's recovery from the current turmoil and may indicate it isn't prepared to pay the price its commitment to a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg will exact. \par \par \uc2  The HKMA - Hong Kong's quasi-\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  - has acknowledged buying Hong Kong dollars three times in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the past week, most recently on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  It claims it's merely selling U.S. dollars from reserves to fund an expected HK$21.4 billion budget deficit for the current fiscal year, and says the moves don't constitute \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But the activity coincided with a build-up of speculative positions against the Hong Kong dollar in the forward market, which boosted the U.S. dollar to HK$7.7510 at one point last week, above the HK$7.7500 level where it typically steps in to cool the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Along with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials have warned they will mount a spirited defense of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although they are secretive about exactly what they will do. \par \par \uc2  "I cannot provide details of the strategies, because when we \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fight against speculators, we would need some element of mystery," Financial Secretary Donald Tsang Yam-kuen said Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  But those threats of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and talk of a mystery arsenal, worry some market participants. They say the HKMA is overstepping its role as a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board custodian and, ironically, giving speculators who hope to break the peg a reason to stick around. \par \par \uc2  "You don't need a secret arsenal if a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board works," says Desmond Supple, BZW's head of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research. "It has domestic economic implications and is particularly alarming to me. That suggests the political will is not strong enough for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board to work." \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 13-08-98 \par \par \uc2  0936GMT \par \par \uc2  1145 4201 62552 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8d06vco\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
